<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137428> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137428 ;
    nex:description "Protected areas (PAs) are the main biodiversity conservation programs and crucial management instruments according to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD). A large body of research has focused on assessing the causal impact of the PAs on biodiversity; however, we still lack the big picture of their results on a global scale. Using studies that employed impact evaluation methods from 2000 to 2023, this study examines the trends related to the effectiveness of PAs on biodiversity loss. We found 129 studies that used impact evaluation methods. The most used method at the country level was matching. Among these studies, deforestation and forest cover were the most frequently examined outcomes, and 99% of these studies used a discrete treatment. Distance, slope, and elevation were widely used as control variables to assess the effectiveness of protected areas. The countries with the highest number of studies were China (23) and Brazil (15). We found that the studies identified did not include biodiversity indicators approved by the CBD, such as the Red List Index or Red Ecosystem List, to assess the effectiveness of PAs. Only one study implemented the Biodiversity Intactness Index (BII) defined in the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework as a potential outcome. Our findings indicate that future research should incorporate biodiversity measures as potential outcome variables when assessing the impact of protected areas. It is also important to consider advances in impact evaluation methods used to assess the effects of conservation programs, such as PAs. In terms of conservation policy, this will offer policymakers decision-making support. This study aims to provide evidence on adopting causal inference theory, outcomes and variables used to assess the effectiveness of protected areas to improve the adoption and evaluation of conservation agreements using impact assessment methods and biodiversity indices" ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "General trends in research using impact evaluation methods on the effectiveness of terrestrial protected areas in reducing biodiversity loss" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1007/s13280-026-02400-3"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137728> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137728 ;
    nex:description "Este artículo estudia parte de las actividades económicas de Nicolás Dávila Maldonado a partir de un análisis detallado de su Libro general de cuentas. Se argumenta que los excedentes generados por la exportación del cacao, producidos en su hacienda Santa Cruz de los Estanques, ubicada en Mérida (actual Venezuela) y cuyos principales mercados eran Veracruz y Cádiz, fueron canalizados en diversas actividades económicas: emisión de créditos, financiación de haciendas ganaderas e, incluso, el sostenimiento de familiares caídos en desgracia. Por medio de las cuentas corrientes que mantuvo con diferentes socios comerciales y familiares, se reconstruye una lógica contable que permite observar la forma en que Dávila articuló redes de circulación de capital que combinaban pagos en efectivo, especie, libranzas y escrituras notariales. El manuscrito propone que no sólo las instituciones religiosas y los comerciantes fueron los únicos oferentes de crédito en las economías preindustriales, sino que los empresarios agrícolas también desempeñaron un papel fundamental en la lubricación de las economías coloniales con numerario." ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Acumulación y crédito en la Nueva Granada en el siglo XVIII: los libros contables de Nicolás Dávila Maldonado" ;
    nex:url "https://alhe.mora.edu.mx/index.php/ALHE/article/view/1601"^^xsd:anyURI .

