<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/138395> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 138395 ;
    nex:description "The long-run effects of conflict on agricultural production depend crucially on how conflict affects farm investment. We use the Colombian peace agreement to estimate how the reduction of violent conflict affects farmer investment decisions. In areas where the armed group was initially present, the end of conflict led to a more than twofold increase in farm investment, as farmers moved production from annual to perennial crops. We find no evidence that investment came at the expense of short-term consumption, and was likely financed by debt. Our results suggest that decreased investment may be an important mechanism through which armed conflict inhibits agricultural development and points to a potentially large peace dividend." ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2023 ;
    nex:title "Conflict and small-scale investment: Evidence from Colombian peace agreement" ;
    nex:url "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/jaa2.46"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/138420> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 138420 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "Since the 1990s, the corporatization of humanitarian aid and the expansion of private donors have filled the gap left by declining government investment in public education worldwide. As these corporate philanthropies take over work once done by governments, they gain spaces of power. This phenomenon has intensified with the global crisis of 2008. The paper asks what are the modalities of domination in corporate philanthropic educational interventions for racialized students in Latin America today, and how the critical literature interprets this phenomenon. It proposes a narrative review that reveals three main analytical trends. First, some studies examine how many corporate philanthropic interventions in education actually have a market colonizing motive. Second, other research focuses on the role of sponsored academic discourses, conventions, networks of influence, and social movements in shaping education policy reforms. Third, research shows that philanthropic efforts, despite their claims to “uplift” racialized students, often fail to disrupt the cycles of exclusion and subalternization they face. The paper concludes by highlighting the need for a broader critical framework for analyzing corporate philanthropic interventions in education, challenging the reductive and paternalistic representations of racialized students that often underlie these efforts, and understanding them within broader historical processes." ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Corporate Educational Philanthropy for Racialized Latin America: A Provocation for More Critical Studies" ;
    nex:url "https://compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/soc4.70081"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/138421> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 138421 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "The PISA test is fundamental to understanding how the concept of educational quality is operationalized, carrying significant normative influence in the global education arena. Beyond its technical function, PISA has become a powerful political tool, establishing the OECD as a central actor in global education governance. This paper uses critical discourse analysis to examine how Agency in the Anthropocene: Supporting Document to the PISA 2025 Science Framework negotiates the tension between two seemingly opposing educational rationales. While existing research has noted PISA’s shift from a focus on human capital and economic growth to broader goals of social inclusion and sustainable development (in line with SDG 4), this paper identifies a further mutation: a growing emphasis on student agency in the Anthropocene aimed at enabling systemic change. This shift leverages global concerns about environmental and social crises to reinforce PISA’s relevance and OECD’s authority in measuring educational quality. The document embraces ecosystemic perspectives and a social reconstruction rationale, framing students as agents of change in addressing climate and economic inequalities. However, despite its progressive discourse, it does not fully abandon the entrenched logic of social efficiency that links quality education to strong PISA performance. The paper concludes with recommendations for aligning OECD practices with its stated vision." ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Educational Quality in Strategic Negotiation: A Critical Discourse Analysis of the Rebranding of the PISA 2025 Science Test Rationale." ;
    nex:url "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/02671522.2025.2534948"^^xsd:anyURI .

