<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/64872> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 64872 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "This article examines how the inhabitants of Putumayo, a department of Colombia both divided and held together licit and illicit authority structures and markets, engage with varied political orders as they seek to advance individual and collective economic and political projects in Colombia’s Amazonia borderlands. We argue that participants in these markets seek to operate between the state and non-state armed actors using the gaps of force and contested terrains of the department to advance future-oriented individual and collective economic and political projects. The nature of each of these markets shapes the interactions between the legitimate and illegitimate armed actors and the population in different parts of Putumayo.  The core of this paper focuses the large coca growing industry in the region. Conversations with numerous coca growers and militants revealed a strikingly consistent set of future-oriented ideologies that focused substantially on how the limitations on state spending limited educational and commercial opportunities.  Coca cultivation, far from being just a peasant survival strategy, was seen by many that we interviewed in both the middle and poorer classes as a way not just of supplementing household income but of providing for the education of children to enable them to reach the middle class and accumulate capital, and generally provide a better life for themselves. At the same time, the illegal nature of the market generates a remarkable amount of anxiety that coca growers manage through a series of different techniques of engaging the state and non-state armed actors protect their coca plantings. We contrast the coca growing economy in Putumayo with the licit petroleum market and the partially legal riverine mining economy.  Here we found that these markets, similarly, provided a basis for thinking about the future and driving local ideology.  Both markets provide many with access to a decent living in a region far from the center of the Colombian economy.  Both markets also create their own anxieties around the effects that the projects have on the environment that Putumayenses live in and, often, cultivate agriculturally. Differences about how to exploit these markets are also resolved often through individuals and collectivities simultaneously engaging with the different armed actors operating in the region.  This paper shows how those inhabiting Colombia’s borderlands seek to make a living and, at times, survive on the land, move deftly in the interstices of both licit and illicit armed power to advance collective and individual economic and political agendas in an effort to build a future that they imagine for themselves." ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2021 ;
    nex:title "Advocacy, Misdirection, Protest and Exit: Strategies of Aspiration and Anxiety amid Crime and Conflict in Putumayo, Colombia. Special Issue: Illegal Futures and Criminalized Markets. Journal of Latin American Politics and Society. University of Miami" .

