<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/96977> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 96977 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description """How are patterns of armed control and dispute by nonstate armed actors related to land concentration and distribution? We argue that nonstate armed actors affect land tenure using different land transfer mechanisms to distribute the land, which reflect the dynamics of control and dispute during civil conflict. We test this argument by studying the case of Urabá, a region in northwestern Colombia, using a mixed-methods strategy. First, using extensive qualitative fieldwork, we find that nonstate armed actors systematically applied different land transfer mechanisms in areas where they held control and where they disputed it with others, which in turn, affected land tenure. Through our fieldwork, we also identify patterns of territorial control by nonstate armed actors
at the village or vereda level. We then analyze an original dataset of rural plot ownership in Urabá, based on official cadastral information. We estimate village-level fixed effects models of land tenure structure that show that disputed territories and those under paramilitary control had larger plots, higher land inequality, and fewer land transfers. In contrast, territories with established control by left-wing insurgents exhibit a small landholder scheme. These results offer important insights into the relationship between civil armed conflict and land, and the logic of rebel governance.""" ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2022 ;
    nex:title "Control, Dispute, and Concentration of Land During Civil War: Evidence from Colombia" .

