<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/104877> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 104877 ;
    nex:description "This chapter explores the transnational history of the Vietnam War in Africa. It argues that, while the conflict in Southeast Asia did not influence politics and societies on the continent in the same ways that it did in much of the West, the war was significant in many African countries and particularly in contexts where politics was imagined in internationalist terms. As such, it has an important history in states that aspired to lead non-aligned worldmaking projects and in southern African societies engaged in struggles over White-minority rule. The chapter, therefore, decentres anti-war protests within the historiography on the Vietnam War to one of four sets of transnational relationships that connected African societies to the war. These relationships include (a) military interactions between the North Vietnamese and African liberation movements, (b) the role that non-aligned African state leaders played in trying to broker peace in Southeast Asia, (c) the history of African anti-war protests in solidarity with the NLF and the North Vietnamese and (d) the solidarities forged between white conservatives in the US and Southern Africa, who imagined the war in Vietnam and the repression of African nationalism as two fronts in a global struggle against communism. In examining these relationships, this chapter both offers a historiographical challenge of the idea that the Vietnam War is a mere footnote in African history and it also opens a vista from which the historiography of the Vietnam War on the continent might be meaningfully developed." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/43302> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2022 ;
    nex:title "The Vietnam War in Africa" .

