<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/105399> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 105399 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "A debated topic in the bilingualism field is if there is an advantage for bilinguals in terms of executive functions (EF). EF are an assemblage of mental processes and self-regulation skills including both basic and higher-level cognitive processes such as inhibition, working memory, monitoring, task shifting, updating, working memory capacity, planning, flexibility, and reasoning. This study investigated whether there was an inhibition control advantage for highly proficient Spanish-English late bilingual adults, compared to Spanish-speaking monolingual adults in Bogotá, Colombia. The study utilized the Victoria version of the Stroop Task (Stroop, 1935) to measure the abilities of inhibitory control between the two groups, in addition to other measures to determine second language proficiency levels. A total of 21 monolingual and 20 bilingual participants between the ages of 20 to 38 took part in the study. Results showed that there was no significant difference in response times between the bilingual and monolingual participants when performing the Stroop task. The implications of these results, the limitations of the study and recommended changes for future studies, which could potentially demonstrate a bilingual advantage, are discussed." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/50703> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2022 ;
    nex:title "The Bilingual dilemma: Inhibiting Interference and its Effect on the Bilingual Advantage" ;
    nex:url "http://doi.org/10.4067/S0718-09342022000200560"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/105402> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 105402 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "The present theoretical study is concerned with the behavior of a  molecular complex strongly confined in a semiconductor quantum ring under external probes: intense laser-field radiation and orthogonal electric and magnetic fields. The theoretical calculations were made using finite element method within the effective mass approximation. The effects of the external probes, donor-donor separation and quantum ring geometrical parameters on the  total energy,  binding energy, and  optical response are studied. We show that the laser-field radiation has a great influence on the two fragmentation processes of  and on their essential features: equilibrium length and dissociation energy. The  optical response defined through adsorption coefficient is studied. Laser field radiation may cause red or blue shifts in the adsorption coefficient peak depending on the quantum ring size and donor-donor positions while the magnetic field can induce an optical transparency effect." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7240> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2022 ;
    nex:title "Intense laser field effect on the  molecular complex strongly confined in a semiconductor quantum ring: Electronic and optical properties" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.optlastec.2022.108134"^^xsd:anyURI .

