<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/110013> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 110013 ;
    nex:description "The addition of encapsulated rejuvenators during the fabrication of asphalt mixtures is a novel self-healing technique that aims at promoting the autonomous sealing of cracks through the release of liquid agents contained in capsules. To assure the effectiveness of this technique, it is necessary that: i) the addition of capsules to the mixture does not compromise the mechanical response and performance of the material, and ii) a minimum number of capsules will get ‘activated’ (i.e., the capsule must be able to release the healing agent). This paper presents an initial evaluation of the mechanical response of a self-healing asphalt mixture with capsules using two-dimensional (2D) computational mechanics through Finite Elements (FE) modelling. FE models of random microstructures of these mixtures were used to assess the effects of the addition of capsules on the dynamic axial modulus of the mixture, and to provide an initial quantification of the number of capsules with ‘activation potential’, based on their location within high-concentration stress paths. The study considered capsules of two different sizes (diameters of 1.0 and 1.5 mm) that were added at two doses (0.5 and 1.0 % by total weight of the mixture). The results show that the addition of capsules does not impact the axial dynamic modulus of the mixture and they suggest that there is a significant number of capsules that could initiate self-healing processes under the stress conditions evaluated." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7655> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2023 ;
    nex:title "Computational evaluation of the mechanical response of self-healing asphalt mixtures with encapsulated rejuvenators" .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/110016> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 110016 ;
    nex:description "A growing body of literature has noted an age pattern in the sharing of false news in social media, with older people sharing more often misinformation than younger users. In this article we supplement this literature by documenting two distinct but complementary phenomena: Facebook users share more content as they get older regardless of whether it is political; and that this increment in sharing activity as age increases is more intense with political and partisan URLs. Based on the Facebook Privacy-Protected Full URLs Data Set, a vast Facebook database with demographic information of those who saw and shared links on Facebook in 46 countries, we investigate the impact of age on link-sharing activity. We found that in 43 countries, the average age of people who shared links was considerably higher than the age of those who saw the links. In a more detailed study, with Facebook users in South America, we find that the average age increases consecutively in the sharing of non-political content, in the sharing of political content, in the sharing of partisan sites and in the sharing of right-leaning partisan sites." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10238> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2022 ;
