<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/110042> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 110042 ;
    nex:description "Encapsulated rejuvenators based on sunflower oil and alginate have gained special attention to promote self-healing in asphalt mixtures. However, evaluating the interaction between capsule and asphalt mortar is crucial because it is the zone where fracture by bitumen oxidative ageing initiates. This study evaluates the effect of an optimised encapsulated rejuvenator on the rheological properties of short-term aged (STA) and long-term aged (LTA) asphalt mortars mixtures. 15 designs of alginate biopolymer capsules with sunflower oil were synthesised under vibrating jet technique  varying the CaCl2 concentration (3 levels) and biopolymer:oil (B:O) ratio (5 levels). The morphological, physical, thermal, and mechanical properties of each capsule were statistically evaluated to select an optimal design. The optimal capsule design was incorporated to STA and LTA FAM cylindrical mixtures, evaluating their mechanical and rheological response under Dynamic Mechanical Analysis (DMA). The main results showed that capsules presented sizes between 1.12–1.64 mm with regular spherical morphology. Capsule design based on 5% wt. of CaCl2  and a B:O ratio of 1:7 presented high encapsulation efficiency and thermal/mechanical stability. Rheological properties of STA and LTA mortars incorporating the optimal capsule design showed i) a softening effect, significatively reducing the dynamic shear modulus at lower temperatures, ii) no significative effect on the susceptibility to permanent deformations, and iii) improvement of the fatigue life." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7655> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2023 ;
    nex:title "Rheological behaviour of aged asphalt mortars containing optimised alginate-based polynuclear capsules to promote asphalt self-healing" ;
    nex:url "http://doi.org/10.1061/9780784484913.013"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/110044> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 110044 ;
    nex:classification "B" ;
    nex:description "How do legislators use social media to cultivate their constituencies in large multimember electoral districts? Colombia's Senate nationwide district offers a suitable case to examine how lawmakers, through their behaviour on Twitter, geographically target their audiences. We employ microblog user geolocation methods which infer locations using textual content in order to identify where Colombian senators' Twitter ‘home’ is. We find that senators' location targeting on Twitter largely mirrors the geographical distribution of their votes. Deviations from that pattern mostly stem from their vulnerability to intraparty competition and the nature of their parties' organisation." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10238> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2023 ;
    nex:title "Legislative Twitter Style: Electoral Vulnerability, Social Media, and Constituency Building in Large Multimember Electoral Systems" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1111/blar.13468"^^xsd:anyURI .

