<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/110820> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 110820 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "Is the secret really an entity that lives outside of the relationship between anthropologist and interlocutor? Does the confession of a secret open the door to knowledge of the culture or of the identity of the social group? Lovell, in her text “When Things Get Personal: Secrecy, Intimacy, and the Production of Experience in Fieldwork” (Lovell 2008), argues that the secret is not ‘a thing’ to which the ethnographer gains access. This is not ‘a thing’ that can be possessed, discovered, or verified. Lovell specifies: the confession of a secret is not the access door to truths of identity, rather, its confession and its hearing open the door to authentic dialogue. Following this position, I will argue that learning a secret is not the fieldwork moment that an anthropologist has been hoping for, and that once instructed they can access the core identity of the group they are studying. Understanding that the secret is a relation that leads to the construction of what I call ‘ethnographic intimacy,’ I will argue that it is through this relation that the partial truth of the ethnographic encounter is built. In other words, I affirm that the secret is a relation that is built over the course of fieldwork, it is a performance that is enacted to give life to ethnographic intimacy–trust between anthropologist and interlocutors. I insist that the confession of a secret is a right of passage into trust, to the ‘ethnographic intimacy’ indispensable for being able to build relations with the people who will serve as Cicero in the pictures of the world (Wittgenstein 2014) that we are interested in learning. In this article, I will attend to the construction of secrets that I have experienced with the lowest to the highest military ranks in my fieldwork with military personnel in Colombia. In the course of the 18 years I have dedicated to understanding soldiers’ unquestionables (Wittgenstein 2014), I have taken part in various performances in which members of the armed forces have ‘confessed’ different secrets. In conversations initiated through the opening ‘this cannot leave here, I’m telling you because of my trust in you’, they have made me participate in their emotions, in accounts of their excesses committed in the region’s conflict, in quotidian practices that they consider problematic, and in overviews of missions that have been highly documented by the media. None of these secrets opened a door to unknown dimensions of the army, but each one of them was indispensable in the construction of relationships with soldiers of various ranks. Each one was necessary to establish ethnographic intimacy. These ‘open secrets’ have not been used for the elaboration of the texts that have resulted from that research. Their role has been other: to make it possible to build a relationship with the soldiers, in which they tell me about their lives, their longings, and their affections. That is to say, the secret and its confession have been part of the bond built during fieldwork, a horizon in which demands for listening and research questions are nurtured. The confession of the secret has not opened a door for me to ‘know more’ about the institution; rather, the performance of confession has been indispensable for the construction of the partial truths (Clifford 1983) to which I referred, and for the choice of tropes and topos in the politics of representation. Thus in this text I will argue that the confession of a secret is a performance in which the interlocutor and the anthropologist each know how to play their role. I argue that this performance is a relation that is indispensable for the construction of the ethnographic intimacy it is necessary to understand and attend to the demands of listening, to construct research questions, and to decide how to shape the practices of writing." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8249> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2023 ;
    nex:title "“This cannot leave here, I'm telling you because of my trust in you”: Confessions and ethnographic intimacy in fieldwork with Colombian soldiers" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1111/aman.13885"^^xsd:anyURI .

