<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/125555> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 125555 ;
    nex:classification "B" ;
    nex:description """In the era of increasing digitalization, organizations are shifting towards investments in digital transformation, relying more on technologies such as artificial intelligence (AI) and virtual reality (VR). Sensory interactions in digital environments are certainly a topic to explore in this evolving context. Compared to sight and sound, other senses like taste, touch, and smell are less incorporated into the ever-growing scope of digitalized user experiences. However, emerging Sensory-Enabling Technologies (SETs) are changing this landscape, facilitating the incorporation of multisensory approaches in digital environments. 
Existing research suggests that SETs can positively impact online behavior, influencing decisions, as well as promoting healthier and sustainable habits. Most SETs commercial solutions still predominantly emphasize visual and auditory dimensions, neglecting the potential of other senses in digital environments. There is a general call for more comprehensive research into how sensory interactions, including taste, touch, and smell, can influence behavior, especially in decision-making processes such as shopping.
Inspired by the above, this research topic entitled “Sensory Interactions in Digital and Virtual Realities - New frontiers for the User Experience”, delved deeper into the role of sensory interactions in digital environments, exploring SETs and their connection mainly to online, but also to offline, experiences. We welcomed research on multisensory simulation in digital contexts and their implications across various sectors like education, healthcare, and retail.""" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/51449> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "Editorial: Sensory Interactions in Digital and Virtual Realities - New frontiers for the User Experience" ;
    nex:url "https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2024.1412810/full"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/125568> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 125568 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "International human rights law has long ignored women’s realities, reproducing an epistemological blind spot that has impacted transitional justice mechanisms such as truth commissions. In the early 2000s, this blind spot was challenged by Peru’s Comisión de la Verdad y Reconciliación (CVR; Truth and Reconciliation Commission). The lessons learned in Peru about including gender in transitional justice processes have nourished the application of transitional justice in Colombia. This article examines the inclusion of a gender perspective in Peru’s CVR and highlights the advances and lessons that inform Colombia’s Comisión para el Esclarecimiento de la Verdad, la Convivencia y la No Repetición (CEV; Commission for the Clarification of Truth, Coexistence, and Non-Repetition). Furthermore, it underscores the innovative contributions of Latin American decolonial and communitarian feminisms to reframing the inclusion of gender in the context of Colombia’s CEV. We argue that these feminisms allow the recognition that “woman” is a plural subject intersected by race and class; challenge liberal feminist conceptions of gender; propose a reconceptualization of time; provide a historical explanation of the reasons behind violence against women; and give an important centrality to nature, territory, and the sacred in the explanation of violence and in peacebuilding." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/38789> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2023 ;
    nex:title "Feminist trajectories from Peru to Colombia: taking violence experienced by women into account in truth commissions" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1080/14616742.2023.2187428"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/125570> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 125570 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "Reflection as a resource and capacity in Social Transformation Design leads to understanding the nature of designing as an emergent communal act. This article explores the polysemic use of reflection and the role that design practitioners and researchers could play in the Global South, and how their work may become a decolonial practice, transitioning from a design-led intervention approach to a collective and collaborative process of joint inquiry. We present a case study on the Colombian Caribbean shores, where People from Ciénaga Grande de Santa Marta and a team of researchers, design and build a palaphitic building for cultural tourism practices. In this article we look at what the process entailed in retrospect to understand how to move from dialectics to dialogics, through a meta-analysis, and not at the actual process of building the palaphitic structure. In our study, we tracked four different categories of reflection, from the conversations with all the participants of the co-creation and co-design activities. We identified two sets of  intertwined results. Firstly, transitional forms of reflection as hybridizations, transitions, and self-contained features in a process that fluctuates from design interventions to joint inquiries. Secondly, how people flow from a dialectical position to a participatory dialogical feeling-thinking-doing. This suggests a way to engage in design practices through meaningful and transformative conversations that allows an understanding of the nature of designing as an emergent communal act in a tropical setting, through tropical vibes." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9697> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "Tropical Vibes with Peoples from Ciénaga Grande de Santa Marta: Dialogic Reflection in Joint Inquiries as Feelthinking-Doing in Design Practice and Research" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1080/15710882.2024.2352717"^^xsd:anyURI .

