    nex:title "Puede haber una Tecnología Liberadora" .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/126720> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 126720 ;
    nex:classification "B" ;
    nex:description "This paper presents the results of an experimental program carried out in the laboratory aimed at assessing the liquefaction resistance by correlations between longitudinal wave (P-wave) and shear wave (S-wave) velocities (VP and VS) and cyclic stress ratio from triaxial testing (CSRCTx) for different degrees of saturation (Sr). The liquefaction resistance was assessed using a cyclic triaxial apparatus equipped with Hall-effect transducers and bender elements, combining stress-based (large-strain level) and wave-based (small-strain level) approaches. These tests were carried out in soil specimens at relatively high degrees of saturation, which were estimated during testing by VP measurements interpreted using Biot’s theory. The results revealed that, for the same relative density and confinement stress, the S-wave-based approach did not predict the liquefaction resistance well because of the negligible variation in the stress state and soil stiffness for the assessed Sr values, which were above the air-entry value. In turn, the P-wave-based approach effectively predicted the liquefaction resistance increment of the TP-Lisbon sand for different Sr conditions because of the strong dependency of P-wave propagation on the degree of saturation in granular media. This is a consequence of the most relevant factor conditioning the pore pressure buildup in partially saturated sands, e.g., the compressibility of the occluded air bubbles, which can be detected by VP but not by VS." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7564> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2023 ;
    nex:title "Experimental Wave-Based Assessment of Liquefaction Resistance for Different Degrees of Saturation" ;
    nex:url "https://api.elsevier.com/content/abstract/scopus_id/85175488218"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/126721> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 126721 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "Diatomites are unicellular algae fossils that can be found worldwide. This paper studies the diatom's shape effect on diatomaceous soil mixtures' dynamic and compressibility properties. Physical characteristics of six diatomite samples with variable shapes and taxonomies are presented. Among these six samples, three diatomites of similar grain size distribution were mixed with kaolin, and the mechanical properties (i.e., shear modulus and damping ratio from small to large strains) were analysed. The results showed that diatom frustules increased the mixtures' compressibility and reduced the maximum shear modulus compared with pure kaolin samples. Moreover, the diatom's shape influenced the normalised shear modulus degradation curve and the damping ratio. Overall, the presence of diatomites reduces the energy dissipation capacity in soils. This research evidenced the importance of distinguishing the shape of diatom in soil mixtures when mechanical properties are analysed, a situation that has yet to be explored." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7564> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2023 ;
    nex:title "Effect of the particle's shape on the dynamic shear modulus and compressibility of diatomaceous soils" ;
    nex:url "https://api.elsevier.com/content/abstract/scopus_id/85171390265"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/126723> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 126723 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10846> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
