<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/129202> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 129202 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "This study explores the impact of peer-to-peer interaction quality (PTPQ) and peace of mind (POM) on customer experience (CX) among Generation Z consumers in retail environments. A theoretical model is proposed, analyzing POM as a mediator in the relationship between PTPQ and CX, while also considering the moderating role of shopping values (hedonic and utilitarian). The findings reveal that PTPQ significantly influences CX by fostering customer confidence through interactions with peers. POM further enhances this relationship by providing emotional reassurance, which is particularly impactful in hedonic shopping contexts. These results underscore the importance of incorporating both controllable and uncontrollable touchpoints in CX design, emphasizing the need for strategies that integrate emotional and social dimensions. The study highlights Generation Z's distinct preferences for trust and connection, offering valuable insights for retailers aiming to enhance customer loyalty and satisfaction through innovative experience management." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/51268> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Do peer-to-peer interaction and peace of mind matter to the Generation Z customer experience? A Moderation-Mediation Analysis of Retail Experiences" ;
    nex:url "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1057/s41270-025-00381-y?utm_source=rct_congratemailt&utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=nonoa_20250204&utm_content=10.1057%2Fs41270-025-00381-y"^^xsd:anyURI .

