<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/129204> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 129204 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "This study comprehensively analyzes a mixed-flow Electric Submersible Pump's (ESP) performance under challenging conditions of low flow rates and high gas fractions. The research leverages a computational model built on the Eulerian-Eulerian methodology, adeptly solving the 3D incompressible Navier-Stokes equations for each phase. To ensure an encompassing depiction of the flow dynamics within the ESP, the model considers critical parameters such as the bubble diameter and interphase forces, including drag, lift, and virtual mass force. Key findings reveal that the ESP's performance deteriorates markedly under the studied conditions. One of the primary influencers is the phenomenon of gas locking, where gas accumulation within the impeller hampers the consistent flow of liquid, thereby degrading the pump's efficiency. In addition, the model delineated the occurrence of the surging phenomenon across diverse gas volume fractions and rotational speeds, which further exacerbates the reduction in the ESP's performance. Intriguingly, the bubble diameter was identified as a decisive factor, especially at minuscule flow rates. Large bubble diameters instigate the formation of gas pockets on the suction side of the vane, culminating in a segregated flow pattern and further declining pump efficiency. On the other hand, the drag force is the primary interaction force between phases of gas-liquid flow. Other interfacial forces, such as lift and virtual mass force, change the pump's performance but do not affect the gas flow and distribution patterns for a one-stage configuration. Results show that the Schiller-Neuman drag coefficient offers a better fit than the Bozano-Dente coefficient. Conclusively, the study underscores the indispensable role of nuanced computational models in elucidating the intricate flow dynamics within ESPs, especially under conditions fraught with operational challenges. The insights gleaned enrich our understanding of ESP performance dynamics and provide a roadmap for designing more resilient and efficient pumping systems in the future." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10151> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "Analysis of two-phase gas-liquid flow in an Electric Submersible Pump using A CFD approach" ;
    nex:url "http://doi.org/10.1016/j.geoen.2023.212510"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/129209> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 129209 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "The increasing pollution of water sources due to industrial dyes, pharmaceuticals, pathogenic microorganisms, and persistent organic and inorganic compounds has necessitated the development of alternative water treatment methods. In this study, silica nanoparticles were synthesized using sugarcane bagasse as a raw material through a green synthesis approach. The silica nanoparticles were functionalized with cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) and characterized using scanning electron microscopy (SEM), Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), X-ray diffraction (XRD), and zeta potential analysis. The antibacterial efficacy of the SiNPs was evaluated against Escherichia coli and Pseudomonas aeruginosa in in vitro tolerance assays. Results demonstrated that SiNPs at concentrations above 500 mg/L significantly inhibited bacterial growth, with performance increasing at higher concentrations. Specifically, P. aeruginosa showed a 96.62% inhibition at 5 mg/L, while E. coli exhibited complete inhibition at 50 mg/L. The minimum inhibitory concentration was determined via broth macrodilution, confirming the antibacterial potential of SiNPs at lower concentrations. This study highlights the potential use of sugarcane bagasse-derived SiNPs as eco-friendly and effective materials for water disinfection applications." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9870> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Biogenic Sugarcane Bagasse-Derived Silica Nanoparticles for Water Treatment: Antibacterial Applications" .

