<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/130382> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 130382 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "Arazá, is an amazonian fruit considered as a good source of bioactive compounds such as ascorbic acid, with a high perishability. To preserve these compounds and their antioxidant activity, its pulp was spray dryed using mixtures of soy protein isolate (SPI) and maltodextrin (MD) as wall materials. A response surface methodology (RSM), applying a Box-Behnken design with three factors, three levels and five replicates at the center point (17 experiments) was used to evaluate the effect of the SPI:MD ratio (0:1 – 1:1), drying temperature (100 – 160 °C) and feed flow (3 – 9 mL/min) in the optimization of the drying process. Yield (Y), moisture (M), water activity (aw), water solubility index (WSI), hygroscopicity, ascorbic acid and phenolics encapsulation efficiency (EEAA, EEP), and the retention efficiency of DPPH and radical scavenging capacity (DPPH, ABTS) were studied as response variables; being Y, M, aw, WSI and EAA the ones considered for optimization purposes. Optimal conditions of SPI:MD ratio (1:1), drying temperature (160 °C) and feed flow (3 mL/min) with low predictive error and high desirability (0.973) were determined based on adjusted quadratic models of response variables, which allowed to obtain microencapsulates with desirable physicochemical properties of moisture (1.37±0.04%), water solubility index (99.21±0.01 %) and water activity (0.08±0.00), as well as high EEAA (71.39±1.31). Morphological analysis showed semi-spherical particles without agglomeration with an average diameter of 7.1 ± 3.4 μm. This study presents an optimized method to obtain microencapsulates from arazá pulp (not obtained before), with a great potential for the development of functional foods." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/60561> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Optimization of the encapsulation of arazá pulp by spray drying: Physicochemical, morphological and in vitro digestion studies" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2772502225001416"^^xsd:anyURI .

