    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2022 ;
    nex:title "Controversias Científicas" ;
    nex:url "https://revistas.unal.edu.co/index.php/idval/issue/view/5825"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/130725> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 130725 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "International literature that addresses local economic development (LED) from a gender perspective is almost non-existent. The dialogue of LED literature with feminist thought has been limited. However, based on the complementarities between these two frameworks, it is possible to explore an analytical approach to local development from a gender perspective with two intermediate goals: locally - induced economic growth and women's empowerment. Therefore, this article proposes a dialogue between local and gender approaches to development by analysing the positive externalities resulting from collective action. Considering a mixed-method approach, this paper analyses the experiences of three Colombian rural associations in triggering local development processes with a gender perspective. Results show that although the collective action of women producer organizations generates progress towards gender equality and empowerment, these achievements are limited, and internalized structural oppressions remain. International programs that catalyse local development and gender empowerment must go beyond promoting access to and control over economic resources and encourage transformative changes through the exercise of the \"power with\" and \"power to\"." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9834> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Linking local and gender development. Women’s organizations in coffee production in rural Colombia" ;
    nex:url "https://www.tandfonline.com/toc/rgab20/current"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/130726> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 130726 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "The lack of anatomical evolution contrasted with an evident behavioral change in humans during their natural history, from about 200,000 to 700,000 years ago, constitutes something of a puzzle. What explains the behavioral change, a change which is commonly understood as cultural? Against the surprisingly widespread but tautological response that the change was driven by culture – which amounts to the unsatisfying argument that culture drives culture, all the way down, or back – this paper presents a theory developed by Andrey Vyshedskiy, whose work on autism and language therapies has led him to an account of the neurobiological basis of voluntary imagination, which here I redescribe as an account of the evolution of the neurology of culture." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8967> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "The neurology of culture" ;
    nex:url "https://brill.com/view/journals/jocc/25/1-2/article-p64_4.xml"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/130727> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 130727 ;
    nex:description "My task in this piece is to give a different but still plausible account of the emergence of the human within the framework of Generative Anthropology. As an author who aspires to publication in Anthropoetics, I hope I am justified in assuming more than superficial familiarity with Generative Anthropology and the Originary Hypothesis on the part of readers. Rather than spend time summarizing the GA basics, then, I will begin by articulating some difficulties I have with some of its presuppositions and conclusions. I do this to clear the ground for the introduction of an alternative hypothesis, based on the work of Andrey Vyshedskiy on prefrontal synthesis (PFS). This hypothesis derives from his work on language disorders, but though it diverges from the OH it still comports well with mimetic theory—as well as Generative Anthropology—in spite of being, apparently, ignorant of both. To be clear then, I am appropriating Vyshedskiy’s hypothesis but rearticulating it in line with GA, which prioritizes the sudden emergence of the human as a point of departure for research in the social and human sciences, and never loses sight of the importance of mimesis for any understanding of the human that so suddenly emerged." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8967> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "Homo imaginatus: Generative Anthropology, prefrontal synthesis and the origin of the human." ;
    nex:url "https://anthropoetics.ucla.edu/ap3001/3001lobo/"^^xsd:anyURI .

