<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/133248> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 133248 ;
    nex:description "¿Por qué un particular que causa un daño a otro debe responder, y por qué debe hacerlo el Estado si el daño lo causa uno de sus agentes? ¿Qué objetivos debe perseguir un sistema de responsabilidad civil extracontractual? Las preguntas anteriores están relacionadas con los fundamentos y las funciones de la responsabilidad civil. Este artículo estudia las teorías que han sido propuestas para responderlas y luego evalúa su posible extensión a la responsabilidad extracontractual del Estado. Se argumenta que el estudio de los fundamentos y las funciones de la responsabilidad civil es un presupuesto necesario para entender —y cuestionar— sus similitudes y diferencias con el régimen de la responsabilidad del Estado. Y, por qué no, para pensar en su eventual unificación." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/59472> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2021 ;
    nex:title "De fundamentos y funciones: ¿un puente entre la responsabilidad civil y la responsabilidad del Estado o más argumentos para el divorcio?" ;
    nex:url "https://anuarioderechoprivado.uniandes.edu.co/images/pdfs/anuario3/03-AD3-Bonivento-30ago2021.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/133262> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 133262 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "The 2024 Venezuelan presidential election, initially conceived as a strategy to regain international legitimacy, marked a turning point in the radicalization of Nicolás Maduro’s regime. This article analyzes how a calculated attempt to simulate competitive elections collapsed under internal and external pressures, triggering the most acute phase of authoritarian consolidation in Venezuela’s recent history. Using congruence-based process tracing, the study examines the causal mechanisms linking the erosion of legitimation and co-optation to the regime’s turn toward systematic repression. Anchored in the theories of competitive authoritarianism, autocratic stability, and democratic rollback, the analysis shows how the regime’s survival logic shifted as opposition mobilization, international scrutiny, and diminishing state capacity undermined its traditional tools of control. The regime’s response—marked by electoral manipulation, post-election violence, and elite securitization—revealed a structural transition from hybrid authoritarianism to full autocratic closure. Rather than a tactical improvisation, chavismo’s radicalization followed a recursive feedback loop of miscalculation and coercive adaptation. The article concludes that Venezuela’s 2024 crisis exemplifies how legitimacy-seeking under authoritarian rule can catalyze its undoing, pushing regimes toward repressive entrenchment when the capacity for political inclusion has been exhausted." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/53752> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Fraud, Miscalculation, and Authoritarian Radicalization in Venezuela’s 2024 Election" ;
    nex:url "http://dx.doi.org/10.4067/s0718-090x2025000200373"^^xsd:anyURI .

