<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/133925> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 133925 ;
    nex:description """Objectives: To estimate incremental healthcare costs associated with COPD using administrative healthcare data in Colombia. 
Methods: We conducted a retrospective cohort analysis using administrative health databases from Colombia’s population covered by the contributory health insurance scheme. COPD patients were identified using a specific operative definition, and the control group was randomly selected from the remaining individuals. To estimate the incremental cost of COPD, we applied a causal approach using a doubly robust model, integrating propensity scores and outcome regression models. This ensured accurate estimation by addressing potential confounding, being robust for misspecification both in the propensity score or in the mean outcome. Confidence intervals were obtained using bootstrapping. In addition, we analyzed the incremental effect of common COPD complications, such as cardiovascular diseases and pulmonary infections. 
Results: Our group of patients with COPD was composed of 251,060 people, whereas our control group was composed of 967,081 people. The estimated incremental cost of a person with COPD was $1,349 USD. The incremental cost of a person with COPD and some type of complication ranges from $1,405 USD to $2,935 USD. The most expensive complication is lung cancer, and the least expensive complication is any type of muscular weakness.
Conclusions: The incremental costs of COPD in Colombia align with the average values typically reported for middle income countries. However, these estimates fall on the lower end of global cost estimations. There was a greater incremental cost found for patients with complications such as lung cancer, cardiovascular diseases and pulmonary infections.""" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10850> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Incremental healthcare costs associated to Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease in Colombia using administrative healthcare data" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S221210992500439X"^^xsd:anyURI .

