<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/133931> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 133931 ;
    nex:classification "C" ;
    nex:description "This article combines poetry and research to prompt reflection on how bureaucratic processes affect families seeking redress for perceived health care failings, using the example of complaint handling by a health care organisation. The contribution emerged in response to my family’s experiences of navigating health providers’ complaints processes. Our complaints were about the late diagnosis of cancer (which came just three weeks before our dad’s death). The article links the experiences depicted in the poem to relevant research literature at the intersection of patient experience and organisational processes. The experience described in the poem fits with the concept of participating in institutions’ “complaint handling” processes, yet a lack of “complaint resolution” on the part of patients / families being achieved. It concludes that qualitative research is well placed to explore how diverse, non-institutionalised forms of patient narrative or expression – whether produced for personal reasons or as attempts to speak to institutions in more creative ways – can be used to challenge the formal, often sanitised, institutional accounts and rationalisations that may, however unintentionally, serve to quieten the voices of those inquiring about, or who once had sought, care." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/61687> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Questioning Care" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10912-025-09988-9"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/133933> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 133933 ;
    nex:classification "B" ;
    nex:description "This article examines the effect of firms’ lobbying expenditures on penalties received from public competition authorities. We show that lobbying expenditures are negatively associated with the amount of the cartel sanction, across and within cartel. Our estimates also report that cartel sanctions are more responsive to lobbying expenditures when the lobbying firm qualifies for the leniency programme. If we consider lobbying expenditures as a proxy for informativeness, more uncertainty in uncovering the cartel incentivizes the antitrust authority to reduce the cartel sanction in exchange for lobby." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/90929> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Lobbying and cartel enforcement" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1080/00036846.2025.2559202"^^xsd:anyURI .

