<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/134169> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 134169 ;
    nex:description "En este artículo examino la influencia del tratado ciceroniano De officiis en las disputas del siglo XVII sobre los fundamentos de la moral, particularmente, en la discusión epistolar entre Hume y Hutcheson, y entre Kant y Garve. Sostengo que, si aceptamos la premisa de que el conocimiento se produce histórica y socialmente, y las relaciones de poder desempeñan un rol crucial en su construcción, es necesario reconocer cómo las teorías morales de Hume y Kant retoman aspectos particulares del tratado ciceroniano tanto para resistir a las instituciones eclesiásticas y académicas que pretenden dictar los modos de hacer filosofía en el siglo dieciocho europeo como para negociar con ellas y hacer de la Ilustración un fenómeno social ampliamente aceptado." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8557> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Kant, Hume y Cicerón: el lugar del poder en las discusiones modernas sobre los fundamentos de la moral" ;
    nex:url "https://dianoia.filosoficas.unam.mx/index.php/dianoia/article/view/2143/2154"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/134180> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 134180 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "Latin America and the Caribbean (LAC) has made significant progress in advancing the Circular Economy (CE) agenda at national levels. Since 2019, eleven LAC countries have proposed National Circular Economy Strategies (NCES) and CE Roadmaps. National Strategies and roadmaps set priorities and propose governance structures and instruments for advancing the CE transition. However, the durable impact of CE policies over time is un- certain due to short-term governance cycles, as the region is marked by challenges in political stability and government changes. While societal transformation towards a CE is expected to take decades, the imbalance between governmental changes and CE policy priority setting emphasizes the need for systems that enhance long-term CE policy resilience and robustness in LAC. This paper identifies factors for enhancing the resilience and robustness of long-term CE policies in the region by applying transition management principles and network governance models. A focus group was developed with CE official representatives involved in the CE policy development in 8 LAC countries—Chile, Colombia, Costa Rica, Dominican Republic, Ecuador, Paraguay, Peru, and Uruguay—and identified in a co-creating process the main challenges and factors to ensure durable impact of CE policies across government transitions in the region. The focus group outcomes were linked to the Worldwide Governance Indicators to assess the political stability of the analyzed countries, providing insights into the CE policy resilience and robustness factors of LAC countries. The outcomes echo practices recommended by tran- sition theory and network governance models, and the adoption of legal backup by higher order laws supporting as instruments for the resilience and robustness of long-term CE policy making." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10861> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Circular economy policy resilience and robustness in Latin America and the Caribbean" ;
    nex:url "doi.org/10.1016/j.jclepro.2025.146755"^^xsd:anyURI .

