<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/135178> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 135178 ;
    nex:description "Toll road projects are employed in developing countries to deliver critical infrastructure, fostering socioeconomic development. However, these initiatives often face community opposition because of misalignment between perceived local needs and promised benefits. This study investigates how institutional and legal frameworks shape community engagement in toll road projects through a multiple case study methodology. We examine decrees, laws, and regulatory resolutions across the three largest national toll road programs in South America. Three governance archetypes reveal distinctive community engagement postures and gaps: Brazil channels operational voice through user-rights and licensing procedures but engages reactively; Chile embeds early, traceable participation and non-judicial technical forums that de-escalate disputes; Colombia mandates socioeconomic compensation plans yet faces coordination and enforcement hurdles that weaken implementation. Implementation is further constrained by indeterminate timelines for prior consultation and inconsistent enforcement of legal provisions. Findings demonstrate that legal tools are most effective as portfolio (combining prior consultation, compensation/contingency funds, procurement clarity, and tiered dispute mechanisms) to curb opportunism and temporal uncertainty while translating social impact into financed entitlements. To address these challenges, we recommend transitioning toward a triadic governance model that formally integrates impacted groups as internal partners throughout the project lifecycle, institutionalizes technical-social dispute boards for non-judicial enforceable conflict resolution, embeds social obligations as binding contractual deliverables, and utilizes flexible financial levers like compensation funds and concession extensions to reconcile financial viability with legible benefits." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8608> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "More than Infrastructure: How Institutional Frameworks shape Socioeconomic Value in Toll Roads" ;
    nex:url "https://nam10.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fascelibrary.org%2Fjournal%2Fjladah&data=05%7C02%7Cda.penaranda%40uniandes.edu.co%7C658d4ec6f8bc49ca646608dde647d8ad%7Cfabd047cff48492a8bbb8f98b9fb9cca%7C0%7C0%7C638919918457497274%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=jHZW74row3kknhyJL1ILzjxWrlItWr0XNvxvcXLZZ34%3D&reserved=0"^^xsd:anyURI .

