<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137158> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137158 ;
    nex:description "Specific habits and preferences for coffee consumption have been established. However, studies that comprehensively integrate the diverse contextual factors that may simultaneously contribute to the enjoyment of coffee are presently lacking. We present a multi-country citizen-science study designed to identify the determinants of consumer’s coffee liking. A questionnaire was provided to participants in 11 countries, over 5 continents, from 77 different nationalities, and using 7 different languages.  Over the course of nearly a year of data collection, 2,987 validated respondents completed the questionnaire relating to demographics, extrinsic and intrinsic qualities of the coffee consumed, together with several coffee-related habits. Participants were recruited from clients of participating coffee shops via a flyer, as well as with targeted announcements placed in various university campuses; the survey was completed on participants’ personal mobile device. Group-based and information-theoretic exploratory analysis approaches were used, together with a suite of complementary hypothesis-based regression approaches designed to predict coffee liking. The results indicate that coffee is preferred when consumed during spring, in the morning hours, on Wednesdays, when consumed black (the fewer added ingredients, the more the coffee is liked), and when consumed from ceramic cups. Additionally, GenX and GenY like coffee best. Importantly, higher-priced coffee is appreciated significantly more (especially by Baby Boomers), and the best-liking-optimised quantity for consumption settles at around 3–5 cups/day. Our dataset also illuminates several key characteristics for coffee dislike: drinking coffee from a cup with a lid on, drinking during evening time, adding cream to the coffee (especially for evening consumption), as well as adding sugar to one’s coffee, suggesting a potential coping mechanism for bitter-dislike. Moreover, people’s reporting later wake-up times and being a national of a coffee-producing country were related to decreased coffee liking, especially amongst Baby Boomers. These results constitute the first multi-country analysis of coffee liking and the specific factors that influence liking. The findings are discussed with respect to the current multidimensional coffee world context." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/51449> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "A multi-country citizen-science study on what makes us enjoy a cup of coffee" ;
    nex:url "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41538-026-00832-5"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137159> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137159 ;
    nex:description "Este artículo cumple una doble función. Primero, explicita y expande el guión curatorial de la muestra retrospectiva Full Dollar: Proyectos Selectos, enteramente dedicada a mi legado de las dos últimas décadas en intervenciones artísticas derivadas de la antropología, misma que tiene lugar desde diciembre de 2025 hasta mayo de 2026. En segundo lugar, hace un recorrido fundamentado en las cédulas museográficas que cree para la exhibición con la finalidad de brindar, a quienes no puedan visitar la muestra, conexiones entre mis distintos proyectos y las estrategias privilegiadas en su conjunto para dar cuenta de las relaciones entre antropología y arte contemporáneo a partir de mi propia práctica." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/28624> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Full Dollar: Patafísica en un Museo" ;
    nex:url "https://artishockrevista.com/2026/03/06/full-dollar-patafisica-en-un-museo/"^^xsd:anyURI .

