<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137312> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137312 ;
    nex:description "The synthesis of the hybrid organic–inorganic metal halide perovskites (C19H42N)3Pb2Cl7, (C19H42N)3Pb2Br7, (C4H7N3)2Cl2PbCl4, and (C4H7N3)2Br2PbBr4 is reported. These Pb-based structures were obtained using cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) and 2-aminopyrazine (2-APZ) as organic precursors, respectively. Single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis revealed that the asymmetric unit for CTAB-based compounds contains three [(C16H33)N(CH3)3]+ cations and a dinuclear [Pb2Cl7]3− unit composed of two lead-centered coordination polyhedra. In contrast, the 2-APZ-based compounds show [PbCl6]4− octahedra connected through vertex sharing to form layered structures alternating with organic [C4H7N3]2+ cations. In both cases, substitution of Cl by Br produced isostructural compounds, but changed the band gap values: from 3.6 eV to 3.3 eV for CTAB-based perovskites and from 2.5 eV to 2.1 eV for 2-APZ-based perovskites. Despite their lower band gap, the 2-APZ-derived compounds exhibited poor ambient stability. Conversely, CTAB-based compounds, although having a higher band gap, demonstrated enhanced thermal and environmental stability due to their hydrophobic nature. The different molecular sizes between 2-APZ and CTAB created different space between inorganic sheets leading to different quantum confinement. These findings highlight the potential for designing improved materials with both low band gap and high stability through mixed occupancy of different organic molecules." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/43204> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Structural analysis of Pb-based organic–inorganic metal halide perovskites modified with Cetyltrimethylammonium bromide and 2-Aminopyrazine: Effects on stability and band gap" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1387700326005125?via%3Dihub"^^xsd:anyURI .

