<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137559> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137559 ;
    nex:description """Abstract
Fracture processes in asphalt mixtures are one of the primary mechanisms of pavement
degradation. Encapsulated rejuvenators represent a disruptive self-healing asphalt technique aimed
at promoting autonomous crack sealing at an early stage. Although existing studies have provided
promising results, further research is needed to determine the mechanical and environmental field
conditions under which this technique could be effective. This study evaluates the self-healing
capacity of long-term aged asphalt mortars containing multicavity biopolymer capsules filled with
sunflower oil through fatigue–healing–fatigue testing conducted under strain-controlled
conditions. Healing periods of 3 and 24 hours at 22 and 45 °C were evaluated. The material ageing
state and testing method simulate challenging scenarios for capsule activation and healing, such as
advanced pavement service life or light traffic loads. The healing capacity was quantified through
three healing indices related to material stiffness recovery and degradation rate under cyclic
loading. The results suggest that the addition of capsules did not significantly enhance the natural
healing capacity of the asphalt mortar under most testing conditions, highlighting the need to better
identify field conditions where this technique can be effectively implemented in road projects.""" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7655> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Effectiveness of Encapsulated Rejuvenators in Aged Asphalt Mortars using Strain Controlled Fatigue-Healing Testing" .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137560> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137560 ;
    nex:description """Abstract
Encapsulated rejuvenators are a promising strategy to enhance the autonomous healing capacity of asphalt mixtures in flexible pavements. However, the mechanisms governing capsule activation, the quantification of the amount of oil release, and the actual contribution of the technique to mechanical recovery after fracture remain insufficiently understood. This study evaluates some of these mechanisms through a comprehensive multi-level evaluation of the thermo-mechanical activation and healing efficiency of asphalt systems incorporating polynuclear calcium-alginate capsules filled with sunflower oil. The experimental plan was designed to quantify: i) capsule survivability and the amount of oil released under stress conditions representative of compaction and traffic loading, ii) the intrinsic healing potential of an aged asphalt binder with and without rejuvenator in doses representing the potential amount of oil release in actual mixtures, and iii) the healing capacity of a long-term aged asphalt mortar with 0.5% capsules per total weight. The last two phases involved fatigue-healing-fatigue tests at intermediate and high temperatures (i.e., 22 and 45 °C). The results show that capsules withstand compaction conditions with limited premature oil loss (≈11%), and that the oil released by traffic-induced stresses increases with temperature. Moreover, the addition of rejuvenator and capsules enhanced the healing capacity only at the highest temperature for the binder and mortar, thereby confirming the strong temperature dependence of the healing process. These findings provide new insight into the relationships between capsule activation, rejuvenator availability, and healing performance, providing insight into field conditions where this technique could be optimised.""" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7655> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Thermo-Mechanical Activation and Self-Healing Efficiency of Asphalt Materials with Encapsulated Rejuvenators" .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137574> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137574 ;
    nex:description "El desarrollo de las humanidades digitales en América Latina se ha visto obstaculizado por las deficiencias en infraestructura y alfabetización digital en la región, y esto se agrava por la carencia de enfoques multidisciplinarios en las universidades e instituciones. The Programming Historian (PH), un proyecto originado en Canadá en 2007, se ha convertido en una infraestructura global para la creación, investigación y divulgación de contenidos asociados con la formación autónoma de habilidades computacionales realizadas por y para humanistas. En este artículo se exploran los retos y estrategias que han permitido construir una red global de historiadores y humanistas en torno a una publicación de flujos metodológicos que abordan la investigación basada en datos. PH no solo contiene tutoriales, sino que también ha creado una red de práctica que se extiende por múltiples países. Se argumenta que PH ha constituido un modelo de infraestructura, alfabetización metodológica y colaboración para el aprovechamiento de recursos comunes en pos de la creación de conocimiento humanístico. Además, se señala que el modelo de infraestructura de PH puede ser replicado en América Latina y otras regiones del sur global para abordar las dificultades en la construcción de infraestructuras de alto nivel para las humanidades." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/55527> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Infraestructuras globales y alfabetización metodológica en las Humanidades Digitales. El caso de The Programming Historian en español" ;
    nex:url "https://cerac.unlpam.edu.ar/index.php/quintosol/article/view/9731"^^xsd:anyURI .

