<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137831> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137831 ;
    nex:description "This  study  examines  online  hate  speech  directed  toward  two  targets:  Venezu-elan  migrants  and  the  Colombian  National  Police.  It  focuses  on  the  types  of  viral  content  that  generate  hostile  engagement  and  the  discursive  organization  of  the  reactions  that  follow.  We  compiled  the  Corpus  of  Hate-Generating  Posts  and  Reactions  (6,488  texts;  210,458  words)  from  major  social  media  platforms  (2018–2025)  and  analyzed  two  parallel  subcorpora  using  a  corpus-assisted  dis-course  studies  approach.  Results  show  that  hate-generating  posts  recurrently  frame both groups in contexts of conflict or wrongdoing, creating conditions for hostile  reactions.  Reaction  analyses  reveal  distinct  narrative  continua  shaped  by  the social positioning of each target. For Venezuelans, a prototypical target of hate speech, hostility escalates toward exclusion. For the Police, not typically consid-ered  a  hate-speech  target,  hostility  culminates  in  delegitimization  and  calls  for  punishment.  The  comparison  demonstrates  that,  despite  differences  in  legal  or  social vulnerability, both groups are subjected to discursively patterned forms of hate speech. We also specify the linguistic resources through which the continua are realized and interpreted. The study contributes to a broader understanding of how hate operates across different social targets in digital environments." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9480> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Discursive-Narrative Strategies in Online Hate Speech Against Venezuelan Migrants and Colombian Police" ;
    nex:url "https://revistas.udea.edu.co/index.php/ikala/article/view/362707"^^xsd:anyURI .

