<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/138281> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 138281 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description """Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly influencing health professions education, including the assessment of clinical competence. Objective Structured Clinical Examinations (OSCEs), widely regarded as a cornerstone of performance-based assessment, offer multiple opportunities for AI integration, yet the current evidence remains fragmented.

In this scoping review, we systematically mapped the existing literature on AI applications within OSCEs, examining how AI has been implemented, the purposes it serves, and the evidence supporting its use. The review identifies emerging trends in automated scoring, feedback generation, learning analytics, assessment design, and examiner support, while also highlighting important challenges related to validity, reliability, fairness, transparency, ethics, and implementation.

By synthesizing the available evidence, this review provides educators, researchers, and institutions with a comprehensive overview of the current landscape and identifies priorities for future research. Rather than replacing human judgment, AI should be viewed as a tool with the potential to enhance assessment quality, support decision-making, and strengthen competency-based medical education when implemented thoughtfully and supported by robust evidence.""" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/74841> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Mapping artificial intelligence integration in objective structured clinical examinations: A scoping review" ;
    nex:url "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/0142159X.2026.2685794#abstract"^^xsd:anyURI .

