<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/138501> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 138501 ;
    nex:description "A chronicle about a research-creation project that explores how bodies become landscapes. Fasciarium (lat. fascia: connective tissue) investigates the rethinking of human identity through fictionalizing the fascia, using costume as a technology to modify, narrate, and put the body into dialogue. The inquiry unfolds through an open-laboratory fiction where participants' own bodies, individual others, and seemingly homogenous group bodies become sites of encounter (to test how self- and other-awareness of the body shape our connection to the world). Costumes act as mediators of these encounters: worn as characters with their own agency, they open conversations between the wearer and their own body, between strangers, and between the individual and the group, allowing participants to speak through appearance where words might not reach. The performance, premiered February 13, 2022, at DAMU's DISK Theater in Prague, is one outcome of this research: a participatory visual and auditory landscape, where audiences transform their appearance through curated or self-assembled textiles. Across the project, the body operates simultaneously as concept, tool, canvas, and character -the coexistence of its biological and social dimensions generating a productive interval between curiosity and discomfort, and a shared language through which strangers can meet-. Designing and wearing garments becomes a method of finding corporeal narratives, using costume as a performative device that stages dialogue and builds dramaturgy." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7483> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2022 ;
    nex:title "FASCIARIUM (lat. for connective tissue)" ;
    nex:url "https://www.resad.com/Acotaciones.new/index.php/ACT/article/view/615/752"^^xsd:anyURI .

