<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/138555> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 138555 ;
    nex:description "The crystal structures of two polymorphs of lapachol [2-hy­droxy-3-(3-methyl-2-butenyl)-1,4-naphthalene­dione, C15H14O3] – a natural pigment relevant in traditional medicine and of considerable scientific interest due to its broad spectrum of biological and pharmacological activities – one with triclinic (P1)and the other with monoclinic (P21/c) space-group symmetries have been redetermined and are compared with a newly identified form, also monoclinic. The three crystal structures are polytypes since they share dimeric layers of hydrogen-bonded lapachol molecules, which connect through π-stacking within a layer. Adjacent layers in the three compounds are held together by weak van der Waals forces between allylic side chains. However, they adopt distinct stacking modes: successive layers are translated along the c axis in the triclinic form, translated and rotated 90° along the b axis in the first monoclinic form and translated and rotated 90° along the a axis in the new monoclinic form. These variations in interlayer arrangement, arising from different relative orientations of interacting allylic moieties, result in subtle differences in the density of the three forms." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/43204> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Three polytypes of lapachol, a natural pigment with promising pharmacological properties" ;
    nex:url "https://journals.iucr.org/paper?aw5103"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/138556> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 138556 ;
    nex:description "The regioselective alkylation of 3-(methylthio)-1H-1,2,4-triazol-5-amine (1) with benzyl bromide (2) promoted by Cs₂CO₃ in dimethylsulfoxide afforded a mixture of two N-benzylated regioisomers: 1-benzyl-3-(methylthio)-1H-1,2,4-triazol-5-amine (3) and 1-benzyl-5-(methylthio)-1H-1,2,4-triazol-3-amine (4). At 293 K, the regioisomers 3 and 4 were formed in a 79:21 ratio, which shifted slightly to 74:26 at 333 K. To elucidate the origin of this regioselectivity, an integrated experimental and theoretical analysis was conducted. Single-crystal X-ray diffraction of product 3 revealed a supramolecular arrangement stabilized by strong N–H···N hydrogen bonds, whose nature and contribution were further analyzed using QTAIM topological methods, Hirshfeld surface mapping, and energy-framework calculations. Complementary physicochemical characterization of both regioisomers was achieved by FT-IR and NMR spectroscopy, high-resolution mass spectrometry (HRMS) and thermal (DSC/TGA) analyses. Additionally, density functional theory (DFT) calculations were employed to analyze frontier molecular orbitals, global reactivity descriptors, and the prototropic tautomerism of precursor 1, providing molecular-level insight into the observed regioselectivity. Gas-phase calculations indicated that the tautomer 1′ is intrinsically more stable; however, PCM model for dimethylsulfoxide simulations demonstrated that strong solvent–solute electrostatic and hydrogen-bonding interactions invert this preference, stabilizing the tautomer 1. Boltzmann population analysis at 293 and 333 K produced tautomeric ratios that closely matched the experimental product distributions, confirming that the formation of regioisomers 3 and 4 is governed by the solvent-dependent 1 ⇆ 1′ tautomeric equilibrium. This integrated experimental–computational approach provides a coherent mechanistic explanation for the observed regioselectivity and delivers a comprehensive structural, electronic, spectroscopic, spectrometric, and thermal characterization of the resulting regioisomers 3 and 4." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/43204> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "N-Benzylation of 3-(Methylthio)-1H-1,2,4-triazol-5-amine Governed by Solvent-Stabilized Tautomerism: Crystallographic and DFT Insights" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022286026004138"^^xsd:anyURI .

