<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/138701> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 138701 ;
    nex:description "This study evaluates the effectiveness of a cementitious mortar overlay reinforced with galvanized graduated wire mesh (GWM) as a low-cost retrofitting solution for unreinforced masonry (URM) walls constructed with horizontally hollow clay bricks. An experimental program involving 56 specimens was conducted, including prisms, panels, and beams subjected to axial compression, diagonal tension (shear), and out-of-plane cyclic bending, respectively. Four configurations were investigated: URM, plain mortar overlay (PM), mortar overlay reinforced with welded wire mesh (WWM), and mortar overlay reinforced with GWM. The results indicate that the contribution of GWM to axial compression was limited, with compressive load capacity similar to that of URM specimens. Under diagonal tension, GWM-retrofitted panels achieved a 92% increase in peak shear load, a 25% increase in shear strength, and a 9.9-fold increase in ultimate shear strain compared with URM panels. Under out-of-plane cyclic bending, GWM-retrofitted beams developed flexural capacities up to 5.7 times greater than those of URM specimens and exhibited a ductility index of 17.4, approximately twice that of WWM-retrofitted beams and nearly five times that of PM specimens. GWM specimens also showed the highest energy dissipation, reaching values up to 114 times greater than those of URM specimens. Although WWM and GWM provided comparable improvements in strength and stiffness, GWM demonstrated superior deformability  and energy dissipation capacity. These findings demonstrate that GWM is a technically viable and cost-effective alternative to conventional WWM for seismic retrofitting of existing masonry buildings." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10104> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Out-of-plane cyclic bending tests of horizontally hollow masonry retrofitted with galvanized graduated wire mesh" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352710226019716"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/138702> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 138702 ;
    nex:description "This article examines Laboratorio de Futuros: Pabellones Radicales, a postgraduate workshop held in Mar del Plata (Argentina) that brought together architecture and design academics and students from Argentina, Chile, and Colombia to experiment with an Epimethean approach to design—acting after rather than ahead of action—, embracing delay, reflection, and care in response to Promethean solutionism. The paper argues that re-situating design as attunement enables new relational infrastructures for multispecies coexistence. It makes four contributions: (1) conceptually, it reframes the pavilion as a host rather than an object; (2) methodologically, it articulates a light, embodied device structured around opening the body, prototyping with remnants, and rehearsing choreographies of care; (3) pedagogically, it positions learning as inter-attunement with territory; and (4) ethicallypolitically, it advances design as a non-accumulative, cosmopolitical mediation practice. Through five situated prototyping cases developed along the Mar del Plata coast, the article presents how affective, and interrelated infrastructures can render wind, erosion, and more-than-human agencies perceptible. Rather than offering replicable solutions, the paper proposes transferable heuristics for designing with planetary excess and damaged worlds." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10444> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Winds, Sand, Companions, humans: Attuning bodies for postantropocentric Worlds" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.21606/drs.2026.1611"^^xsd:anyURI .

