<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/139001> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 139001 ;
    nex:description "The growing urgency of sustainable development has increased the need for new ways to create value that balance economic, social, and environmental priorities. Traditional business models often focus on short-term profits while sustainable development requires long-term strategies that generate value for stakeholders, communities, and ecosystems. Although companies are now committing to sustainability, and existing literature has established clear links between sustainability and performance, critical gaps remain in how enterprises implement these approaches. Specifically, there is a lack of scalable frameworks, standardized metrics, and sector-specific guidance for operationalizing sustainable value creation at the enterprise level. This paper addresses these implementation challenges by examining how businesses can create and apply value-creation methods that support the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). The study explores the drivers, frameworks, and best practices for incorporating sustainability into value creation, examining how organisations can link profitability with positive social and environmental impacts through strategies such as circular economy, shared value, and stakeholder capitalism. Using a convergent mixed-methods systematic literature review approach, we combine academic literature with case studies of profit-seeking organisations that have implemented sustainable value creation, revealing both practical challenges and opportunities. The analysis of 116 scholarly publications and 32 case studies (covering 199 enterprises across 27 countries and 23 industries) identifies three critical gaps in the literature: theoretical fragmentation across disciplines, insufficient frameworks tailored to enterprise contexts, and persistent disconnects between theory and practice. This research offers an integrated, enterprise-level framework for sustainable value creation, along with practical insights for leaders seeking meaningful change." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/90930> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Value Creation for Sustainable Development at Enterprises in Sustainable Development" ;
    nex:url "http://doi.org/10.1002/sd.71668"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/139002> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 139002 ;
    nex:description "In this article we study Figalli and Gigli’s formulation of optimal transport between non-negative Radon measures in the setting of metric pairs. We carry over classical characterisations of optimal plans to this setting and prove that the resulting spaces of measures, $\\mathcal{M}_p(X,A)$, are complete, separable and geodesic whenever the underlying space, $X$, is so. We also prove that, for $p>1$, $\\mathcal{M}_p(X,A)$ preserves the property of being non-branching, and for $p=2$ it preserves non-negative curvature in the Alexandrov sense. Finally, we prove isometric embeddings of generalised spaces of persistence diagrams $\\mathcal{D}_p(X,A)$ into the corresponding spaces $\\mathcal{M}_p(X,A)$, generalising a result by Divol and Lacombe. As an application of this framework, we show that several known geometric properties of spaces of persistence diagrams follow from those of $\\mathcal{M}_p(X,A)$, including the fact that $\\mathcal{D}_2(X,A)$ is an Alexandrov space of non-negative curvature whenever $X$ is a proper non-negatively curved Alexandrov space." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/124371> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Optimal partial transport for metric pairs" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1007/s12220-026-02527-5"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/139003> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 139003 ;
    nex:description "The intrinsic timed-Hausdorff distance between timed-metric-spaces, first introduced by Sakovich–Sormani, yields a weak notion of convergence for space-times. In this paper we prove a compactness theorem for the intrinsic timed-Hausdorff convergence of timed-metric-spaces using timed-Fréchet maps. Our proof introduces the notion of “addresses” and provides a new way of stating Gromov's original compactness theorem for Gromov–Hausdorff convergence of metric spaces. We also obtain a new Arzelà–Ascoli theorem for real valued uniformly bounded Lipschitz functions on Gromov–Hausdorff converging compact metric spaces. Moreover, we establish the triangle inequality for the intrinsic timed-Hausdorff distance." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/124371> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Gromov's Compactness Theorem for the Intrinsic Timed-Hausdorff Distance" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.difgeo.2026.102364"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/139004> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 139004 ;
    nex:description "In this paper we study the isometric rigidity of certain classes of metric spaces with respect to the $p$-Wasserstein space. We prove that spaces that split a separable Hilbert space are not isometrically rigid with respect to $\\mathbb{P}_2$. We then prove that geodesic rays are isometrically rigid with respect to $\\mathbb{P}_p$ for any $p\\geq 1$, whereas taking infinite half-cylinders (i.e. product spaces of the form $X\\times [0,\\infty)$) over compact non-branching geodesic spaces preserves isometric rigidity with respect to $\\mathbb{P}_p$, for $p>1$. Finally, we prove that spherical suspensions over compact spaces with diameter at most $\\pi/2$ are isometrically rigid with respect to $\\mathbb{P}_p$, for $p>1$." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/124371> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Isometric Rigidity of Metric Constructions with respect to Wasserstein Spaces" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jfa.2026.111415"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/139005> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 139005 ;
    nex:description "Persistence diagrams are objects that play a central role in topological data analysis. In the present article, we investigate the local and global geometric properties of spaces of persistence diagrams. In order to do this, we construct a family of functors $\\mathcal{D}_p$, $1\\leq p\\leq \\infty$, that assign, to each metric pair $(X,A)$, a pointed metric space $\\mathcal{D}_p(X,A)$. Moreover, we show that $\\mathcal{D}_{\\infty}$ is sequentially continuous with respect to the Gromov-Hausdorff convergence of metric pairs, and we prove that $\\mathcal{D}_p$ preserves several useful metric properties, such as completeness and separability, for $p\\in [1,\\infty)$, and geodesicity and non-negative curvature in the sense of Alexandrov, for $p=2$. For the latter case, we describe the metric of the space of directions at the empty diagram. We also show that the Fréchet mean set of a Borel probability measure on $\\mathcal{D}_p(X,A)$, $1\\leq p\\leq \\infty$, with finite second moment and compact support is non-empty. As an application of our geometric framework, we prove that the space of Euclidean persistence diagrams, $\\mathcal{D}_p(\\mathbb{R}^{2n},\\Delta_n)$, $1\\leq n$ and $1\\leq p< \\infty$, has infinite covering, Hausdorff, asymptotic, Assouad, and Assouad-Nagata dimensions." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/124371> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "Metric Geometry of Spaces of Persistence Diagrams" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1007/s41468-024-00189-2"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/139006> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 139006 ;
    nex:description "We study the Gromov–Hausdorff convergence of metric pairs and metric tuples and prove the equivalence of different natural definitions of this concept. We also prove embedding, completeness and compactness theorems in this setting. Finally, we get a relative version of Fukaya's theorem about quotient spaces under Gromov–Hausdorff equivariant convergence and a version of Grove–Petersen–Wu's finiteness theorem for stratified spaces." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/124371> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "Gromov-Hausdorff convergence of metric pairs and metric tuples" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.difgeo.2024.102135"^^xsd:anyURI .

