<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/140348> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 140348 ;
    nex:description "El artículo recorre algunos momentos clave de la historia compartida por el arte y el cine con el objetivo de señalar los lugares más destacados de su encuentro y de su re-cíproca influencia, que identifica en la citación, en el uso y adaptación de formatos y, finalmente, en el valor signi-ficante del montaje. El texto utiliza ejemplos tanto loca-les como internacionales para ilustrar situaciones útiles para construir una mirada panorámica. El propósito de la reflexión  es  demostrar  que  el  diálogo  entre  arte  y  cine,  lejos de ser fácil y pacífico y de evidenciar una jerarquía establecida, revela en cambio un conflicto constante en la  lucha  por  cautivar  públicos,  repartirse  espacios  y  si-tuarse en el contexto." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9285> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Arte y cine: lazos de familia" ;
    nex:url "https://revistas.uniandes.edu.co/index.php/hart/issue/view/779"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/140349> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 140349 ;
    nex:description "This article rethinks intergenerational trauma through the problem of directionality and the social forms through which war memories circulate. Scholarship on intergenerational trauma has often relied on a descendant, family- and blood-based model in which traumatic content travels vertically from older ‘carriers’ to younger ‘recipients’. Drawing on Colombian post-conflict materials on ex-combatants’ family ties – especially mothers’ experiences around recruitment, disappearance, reunion, stigma and responsibility, as well as adoptive and extended kin configurations shaped by war – this article argues that traumatic memory frequently circulates relationally and multidirectionally, including ascendant trajectories (from children to parents) and transfilial trajectories (beyond consanguinity and nuclear kin). Conceptually, the article shifts the analytic focus from transmission as inheritance/succession to circulation as role-based, mediated and politically contested. It further situates intergenerational trauma within a broader ecology of traumatic circulation by clarifying its relationship to secondary and vicarious traumatisation, and by foregrounding the work of silence, secrecy and affective responsibility in shaping what can and cannot be remembered. The article concludes by proposing a vocabulary for analysing war memory beyond unidirectional descent, with implications for memory research in other post-violence settings." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7160> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Intergenerational trauma ‘in reverse’: A critique of the modes of circulation of combatants’ memories in Colombia" ;
    nex:url "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/17506980261488740"^^xsd:anyURI .

