<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/140419> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 140419 ;
    nex:description "Catastrophic health expenditures (CHE) are a key barrier to healthcare access for vulnerable populations like migrants. Despite the scale of the Venezuelan migrant crisis, little research has examined health spending for this population, especially among women and children. This study addresses this gap by analyzing healthcare costs and a proxy for CHE among insured and uninsured female Venezuelan migrants residing in Colombia. Using telephone survey data, we examine healthcare utilization and out-of-pocket spending across five healthcare visits: adult visits, child visits, and pregnancy-related visits, including antenatal, postnatal, and delivery services. Additionally, we use logistic regression to examine individual-level predictors of direct medical proxy CHE among adult female migrants (N = 481). We find that uninsured versus insured individuals are less likely to access adult and child healthcare (52% vs. 89% and 69% vs. 84% for adult and child health, respectively; p < 0.001). Uninsured migrants also report higher average healthcare costs, particularly for direct medical expenses, leading to a 33% increased risk of proxied CHE. For all migrants, direct non-medical costs and indirect costs create a substantial financial burden, regardless of insurance status. While costs for pregnancy-related services are comparable for uninsured and insured migrants, uninsured women faced higher direct non-medical costs for delivery (p < 0.05). The results highlight Colombia’s progress toward equitable healthcare access, especially for maternity care, and underscore the need for additional policies to expand health insurance enrollment and address persistent structural and financial barriers to ensure access for migrant populations." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8662> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Healthcare costs and catastrophic health expenditures among female Venezuelan migrants in Colombia" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2949856226001625?via%3Dihub"^^xsd:anyURI .

