<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/140430> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 140430 ;
    nex:description "The slow adoption of wagon transport in the Andes is a puzzle. We examine it by developing a threshold technology-adoption model calibrated using human and mule energy consumption and unique historical data from a human trail and the first wagon road in the Andes. Model predictions align with historical social choices. Pre-Columbians selected a straight, steep, short route for human porters to reduce energy costs, while the Spanish used it with mule packs. Late nineteenth-century transport demand growth surpassed the model’s threshold and contributed to construction of the zigzagging, gentle, long wagon road route, as well as incentives to build state capacity." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8074> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Why Was the Wheel (Not) Used in the Andes? A Hypothesis Explaining the Slow Adoption of Wagon Transport and Evidence from Nineteenth-Century Colombia" ;
    nex:url "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-economic-history/article/why-was-the-wheel-not-used-in-the-andes-a-hypothesis-explaining-the-slow-adoption-of-wagon-transport-and-evidence-from-nineteenthcentury-colombia/F68D06FDF4D2812B59B3994CAD83987C"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/140440> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 140440 ;
    nex:description "The 2016 Peace Agreement between the Colombian Government and the FARC guerrilla group marked a historic step towardending more than five decades of armed conflict. It also reshaped national food policy with lasting implications. During the ne-gotiations, deep ideological divisions emerged between food sovereignty and food security, and these tensions threatened to stallprogress. Yet, negotiators converged on an unexpected overarching frame: the right to food. This study examines the debates andtensions that produced this outcome and shows how the right to food helped bridge entrenched divides. Drawing on qualitativedocument analysis and process tracing of previously classified and public documents, including internal working papers, civilsociety proposals, official statements, and negotiation records, as well as interviews with negotiators, the study traces a 12-yeartrajectory of food policy debates across the phases of exploration (2012), negotiation (2013–2016), and implementation of thepeace accord (2016 to date). The findings suggest that this shift did more than enable agreement at the table. It also influencedimplementation choices and helped open new policy pathways beyond the accord itself, including the later constitutional recog-nition of the human right to food in Colombia" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/83770> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "The Making of the Right to Food: Tracing Food PolicyConflict and Negotiated Outcomes in Colombia" ;
    nex:url "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/epdf/10.1002/wfp2.70030"^^xsd:anyURI .

