<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/55169> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 55169 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "This essay examines Ariston of Chios' doctrine of indifference in light of his comparison of the Stoic sage to an actor who can play any role (πρόσωπον, persona) equally well. For Ariston, it is the non-identification of actor and persona that is the demonstration of Stoic virtue. Contrary to Cicero's criticism, this \"impersonal\" ethical stance does not make deliberation impossible, but it does devalue moral action in favor of the cultivation of virtue as pure capacity or hexis. Ariston can thus be considered as the first expression of a particularly radical form of Stoic thought that finds echoes in later thinkers such as Epictetus." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/27247> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2020 ;
    nex:title "Ariston of Chios and the Sage as Actor: On the Stoic Concept of Persona" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.5840/ancientphil20204019"^^xsd:anyURI .

