<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/62364> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 62364 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description """Carta de aceptación:

Expéditeur: Heski Bar-Isaac <heski.bar-isaac@rotman.utoronto.ca>
Date: 6 août 2019 à 17:39:29 UTC+2
Destinataire: "Jerome Pouyet" <pouyet@essec.edu>
Cc: heski.bar-isaac@rotman.utoronto.ca
Objet: JINDEC: decision on your submission
Répondre à: Heski Bar-Isaac <heski.bar-isaac@rotman.utoronto.ca>

Dear Jerome:

I am happy to accept the paper. I think the changes helped clarify the assumptions and their applied relevance to me, and the paper reads well.

Below is the formal, journal form letter.

Best,

Heski


I am pleased to inform you that I have decided to accept your submission,
MS 2018127, entitled

"Controlling Sellers Who Provide Advice: Regulation and Competition"

for publication in Journal of Industrial Economics.

I would like you to prepare a finalized version of the manuscript for typesetting. What I need from you now is an electronic copy of the final, accepted version ( Emailed to jan@jindec.org), preferably in MS Word or Latex.

Please put any supplemental materials (for publication on the Journal's website but not in the Journal itself) onto a separate file.

      The final version should be as clean as possible so as to speed the process of getting it to print and likewise to avoid introducing new error, which is always a possibility if the typesetters have to go into the document to change something.

      First of all, please go to the Journal's website at <http://www.jindec.org/page/guildelines> for instructions; you will also find there a check list to help you verify that you have followed the instructions.  Final versions that are not to style, not internally consistent, cause a lot of trouble in the final stages of production. Let me therefore make a few simple points.

      As you go along, study carefully a printed article in the Journal, and make your paper look like that. Make the title page look exactly like a title page in the Journal. Bear in mind that the Journal does not use bold type, ever. Therefore, kindly do not use bold type.

      Study the style for section heads and subheads. What is italic on the page, you should make italic; what is in capitals, you should capitalize; what is indented, centred or ranged left, you should indent, centre or range left. What is in Roman numerals on a Journal page should be in Roman on yours. Note that the first paragraph following a head or subhead is not indented.

      Be consistent with punctuation. Remember that we normally use single quotation marks. We only use double quotes for a quotation within a quotation. Be particularly careful to get the style right in the references. It is a rather quirky style that I do not personally like. However, it is Journal style, which you must follow in every particular. I look forward to receiving your paper and taking it to press.

Thank you very much for your contribution to Journal of Industrial Economics.
I look forward to seeing your article appear in print.

Sincerely,

Heski Bar-Isaac
heski.bar-isaac@rotman.utoronto.ca
Journal of Industrial Economics""" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7300> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2019 ;
    nex:title "Controlling Sellers Who Provide Advice: Regulation and Competition" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1111/joie.12228"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/62367> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 62367 ;
    nex:classification "D" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/46332> ;
