<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/74181> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 74181 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "John Searle’s social ontology distinguishes between linguistic and non-linguistic institutional facts. He argues that every instance of the latter is created by declarative speech acts, while the former are exceptions to this far-reaching claim: linguistic phenomena are autonomous, their meaning is “built in,” and this is necessary, Searle argues, to avoid “infinite regress.” In this essay I analyze Searle’s arguments for this linguistic exceptionalism and reveal its flaws. My method is to follow Searle’s argument closely and comprehensively so as to avoid, insofar as is possible, a selective reading of his argument in my favor. Against Searle’s position, I argue that linguistic phenomena are not exceptions to his general theory of institutional facts, for they too always require supplementary representations in order to exist. Language itself is analogous to all other institutions and infinite regress turns out to be unavoidable." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8967> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2021 ;
    nex:title "A Critique of Searle’s Linguistic Exceptionalism" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1177%2F0048393121995581"^^xsd:anyURI .

