<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/79397> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 79397 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "The recent literature on initial public offerings (IPOs) suggests a significant effect of tone in IPO underpricing but is silent about its determinants. This study concentrates on the factors that shape the tone of the information disclosed in IPOs. Using a sample of 211 Latin American IPOs during the period 2000 – 2019, we find empirical evidence that a powerful CEO can influence tone, avoiding unfavorable tone and fostering the use of positive words in the information disclosed to the market. We also show that boards that are more independent tend to use a more unfavorable tone. In addition, we find a non-monotonic relationship between board size and the tone in the prospectus, which suggests an optimal board size that mitigates the excessive use of positive tone and leads to use of a more unfavorable tone in the IPO prospectus. Overall, well-functioning boards counterbalance powerful CEOs and generate a more realistic disclosure to the market. Finally, we found that market-dominant auditors, age of the issuing firm, proposed use of proceeds, and the number of risk factors significantly affect the tone in information disclosed through the prospectus." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8532> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2021 ;
    nex:title "Determinants of corporate tone in an initial public offering: Powerful CEOs versus well-functioning boards" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ribaf.2021.101481"^^xsd:anyURI .

