<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/87952> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 87952 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "This article explores whether the evolution and growing importance of Chinese foreign direct investment (FDI) in Latin America in the 21st Century has exacerbated its rivalry with the United States. Drawing upon heterodox International Political Economy and Latin American approaches to autonomy, we challenge Cold War narratives, as well as conventional interpretations of China's role in the region. We provide quantitative evidence of the existence of three historical waves of Chinese investment and identified trends and the evolution of flows, sectors, firms, and projects. The first wave (2000–2009) comprises investment towards commodities, energy, and transportation infrastructure. The second wave (2009–2019) marks an increase in FDI flows and diversification towards more technologically advanced sectors such as automotive, Information and Communication Technologies (ICT), and renewable energies. The third wave (2019–ongoing) corresponds to the digitisation of the world economy, the emergence of 5G technology, and the beginning of new power relations. Furthermore, we identified Latin American public opinions and perceptions of Chinese investments and discussed its geopolitical implications. We propose that the evolution of China-Latin American relationship epitomises broader transformations of the world order, a technological evolution, and geographical restructuring of global capitalism rather than the resurgence of great powers bipolarity." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/56091> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2021 ;
    nex:title "The Evolution of Chinese Investment in Latin America and the Digital Cold War Narrative" .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/87975> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 87975 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "We assessed negative bias in Colombian young offenders towards affective deviants (those who violate emotional norms). Postulating that affective deviants elicit an “uncanny/creepy” feeling resembling that produced by psychopaths, we explored social judgments of affective deviants in individuals with callous-unemotional (CU) traits. 188 young offenders evaluated a target displaying congruent or incongruent affective displays in response to pictures eliciting positive/negative affect, depending on the condition. Affective deviants were rated as creepier and less trustworthy, and more likely to have bad moral character, than targets who displayed normative affect. Further, affective deviants who displayed positive affect in response to negative stimuli were rated as having worse moral character than those who displayed negative affect in response to positive stimuli. CU traits predicted lower trustworthiness judgments of targets in congruent conditions, but higher trustworthiness judgments of targets in incongruent conditions. CU traits also predicted higher desired social distance and creepiness judgments in congruent conditions. Creepiness ratings correlated with judgments of bad moral character, suggesting that this emotional response may be involved in moral evaluations of strangers. These findings indicate that deviant affective displays produce a variety of negative social judgments, with CU traits playing a role in these social cognitive heuristics." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/28782> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2021 ;
    nex:title "The “Joker” laugh: Social judgments towards affective deviants in a sample of young offenders with callous unemotional traits." .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/8798> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 8798 ;
    nex:classification "C" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8532> ;
