<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/110962> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 110962 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "Geopolymer concrete is an innovative material which offers environmental advantages in comparison with traditional cement systems. Extensive research has been performed on the structural behavior of reinforced geopolymer concrete members; however, to the authors’ knowledge, its shear behavior has not been comprehensively addressed to date. This study develops a backbone model comprising a set of empirical equations for predicting the shear behavior of reinforced geopolymer concrete panels subjected to monotonic diagonal tension. The model describes a trilinear envelope which allows to predict the shear strength and shear drift at three limit states of geopolymer concrete panels, i.e., diagonal cracking, peak shear strength, and ultimate shear strength. Results of a comprehensive experimental program comprising diagonal tension testing on 27 housing panels were considered for the model calibration, including three different concrete types and three web shear reinforcement ratios made of welded-wire mesh. A statistical analysis demonstrated a trustful fitting of the proposed backbone model here, being a useful reference in a new preliminary performance-based framework for earthquake-resistant design of low-rise geopolymer concrete walls controlled by shear deformations." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10021>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10104> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2023 ;
    nex:title "Backbone model for predicting the shear behavior of geopolymer panels under diagonal tension tests" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.conbuildmat.2023.132454"^^xsd:anyURI .

