<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/128238> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 128238 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "This study introduces a novel methodology for evaluating volatile-compound-based compatibility in 160 botanicals essential for gin production. It establishes a comprehensive botanical pairing system through network analysis, mathematical modeling, and indicator development. Key findings include the benefits of strategic botanical inclusions to recipes for compatibility improvement and a numerical compatibility assessment, revealing an average indicator of 1.78 across thirty validated gin recipes, with a significant threshold at 1.36 indicating superior compatibility. Additionally, two distinct indicators were formulated—one focusing on the percentage of shared compounds due to a specific botanical and another on the botanical’s representation in the recipe. A visual network analysis replaces the traditional aroma classification wheel, offering a comprehensive view of aromatic profiles. This research transforms gin formulation, supported by an extensive database and validated methods." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10151>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7103> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "Assessing botanical compatibility in gin production: A mathematical model and network analysis approach" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0960308524000610"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/128244> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 128244 ;
    nex:description "In oil well operations, accurate modeling of two-phase liquid-gas flow in Progressive Cavity Pumps (PCPs) is vital. This study aimed to comprehensively analyze such flow in a metal-metal PCP using computational fluid dynamics (CFD) simulations validated by experimental data. The methodology applied to the High-Resolution Interface Capturing (HRIC) scheme is to compute the convective transport of phase fractions, yielding insights into the intricate dynamics of these flows. Validation was achieved using laboratory-acquired data and comparisons with extant publications. The findings confirmed the CFD simulations' alignment with field data for two-phase liquid-gas flows. Gas compression and effective viscosity gradient were crucial for accurate pump performance prediction. An investigation into the “gas seal” phenomenon using the Eulerian Multiphase Model elucidated the distinct separation of gas and liquid phases. The influence of the gas volume fraction on pump metrics like torque, power, and efficiency was explored, with CFD predictions exhibiting less than 8% error. The study underscores the pivotal role of CFD tools in predicting pump behavior under diverse conditions. It advances our understanding of two-phase flows in oil wells, especially regarding the gas's impact on pump components. This research offers the oil industry a refined modeling tool, enhancing the comprehension of oil well pump dynamics." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10151>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/30001> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "2. Experimental and CFD modeling of a progressive cavity pump (PCP) using overset unstructured mesh part 2 Two-phase flow" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2949891023011880"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/128283> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 128283 ;
    nex:description "By using the tight‐binding scheme with ten orbitals per atom (SP3S* and two spin orientations) the electronic projected bulk band structure, surface band structure (both anion‐and cation‐terminated surfaces), and the wave‐vector‐resolved density of states for these II‐VI semiconductors are calculated. A quickly convergent iterative technique is employed for calculating the Green function of the actual semi‐infinite crystal. This calculational method allows to precise very accurately, the energy of localized surface states. All the materials considered in this work present surface states with energies lying in the fundamental gap as well as surface states in the lenses opened in the projected bulk valence‐band continum. The atomic weight of each surface state is determined. The states in the fundamental gap turn out to be ‘bridge‐bond’‐type states for anion‐terminated surfaces and ‘dangling‐bond’‐type states for cation‐terminated surfaces. Copyright © 1990 WILEY‐VCH Verlag GmbH & Co. KGaA" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10202>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9965> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 1990 ;
    nex:title "Electronic Structure of (001) Semiconducting MTe Surfaces (M = Zn, Cd, Hg)" ;
    nex:url "https://api.elsevier.com/content/abstract/scopus_id/84987055158"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/128363> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 128363 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "En este artículo evaluamos la convergencia de las dimensiones de diferentes modelos teóricos sobre evaluación social recientemente integrados en un adversarial collaboration. Para ello hicimos un análisis de convergencia de los ítems empleados para manipular las dimensiones teóricas en 45 estudios  empíricos. A través de varias estrategias de análisis con dos índices de similitud encontramos escasa semejanza entre dimensiones que supuestamente corresponden. Estos resultados tienen importantes implicaciones para el adversarial collaboration que, si bien es un paso importante en el campo de investigación, puede estar incurriendo una jingle fallacy poniendo constructores diferentes bajo una misma etiqueta." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/11127>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8833> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "Evaluating the Convergence of Dimensions Across Social Evaluation Models" ;
    nex:url "https://econtent.hogrefe.com/doi/abs/10.1027/1864-9335/a000557?journalCode=zsp"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/128364> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 128364 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "En este artículo examinamos la validez estructural de la traducción al español de las Viñetas de Fundamentos Morales, un instrumento desarrollado para medir el juicio moral en el contexto de la Teoría de Fundamentos Morales. Con datos de 3 países (N = 1.650, a través de una agencia de encuestas) identificamos un conjunto restringido de ítems que se ajustan a la solución de siete factores implícita en la teoría. Realizamos análisis adicionales (pruebas de invariancia y funcionamiento diferencial de los ítems) para examinar la estabilidad de los resultados entre países. Encontramos no invariancia y funcionamiento diferencial uniforme en un gran número de ítems. En conjunto, estos resultados sugieren que, aunque la versión actual de la MFV puede usarse adecuadamente para medir el juicio moral dentro de las sociedades, las comparaciones transculturales con esta herramienta son restringidas. Nuestra validación plantea preguntas sobre la validez transcultural del instrumento, pero también sobre algunas de las categorías que subyacen a las mediciones previstas." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7076>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8833> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Validation of the Moral Foundation Vignettes in Latin America: The scope of moral foundations through the lens of an instrument" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1525/collabra.128178"^^xsd:anyURI .

