<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137462> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137462 ;
    nex:description "Efficient energy management in microgrids remains challenging due to the stochastic variability of renewable generation and the inherent electrical characteristics of distribution networks. Unbalanced three-phase AC microgrids exhibit complex operating conditions driven by unequal loading, asymmetrical line parameters, and electromagnetic coupling effects, which complicate control design under uncertainty. This work presents a reinforcement learning–based energy management system built upon the Soft Actor–Critic (SAC) algorithm, specifically tailored for unbalanced three-phase microgrids. To ensure compliance with operational limits, a Lagrangian penalty mechanism is incorporated to regulate voltage magnitudes and other technical constraints during training. The proposed approach is benchmarked against a deterministic quadratic constrained programming (QCP)–based energy management strategy under stochastic operating scenarios. Extensive Monte Carlo simulations demonstrate that the SAC-based controller achieves higher typical operational returns while maintaining voltage levels within admissible limits and only marginally increasing phase unbalance compared to the optimization-based baseline. Although the QCP strategy exhibits lower per-step computational times, both approaches remain computationally feasible for practical deployment. These results suggest that reinforcement learning can serve as a flexible alternative for energy management in realistic unbalanced three-phase microgrids operating under uncertainty." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/26581>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/76250> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Soft actor-critic energy management in three-phase unbalanced microgrids with lagrangian penalty constraints" ;
    nex:url "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-026-47679-0"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137471> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137471 ;
    nex:description """Active Mobility To and from School (AMTS) provides critical health and equity benefits, yet interventions in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs) often face significant challenges in transitioning from pilot to routine practice. This paper documents the design of the On the Way to School (OWS) project, which is a South–South collaboration between Colombia and Mozambique. We address the translational gap in transport planning by prospectively applying the Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research (CFIR) to structure a transferable four-step co-design pathway.
The pathway operationalises study creation through: (i) site-specific problem framing; (ii) mapping implementation determinants; (iii) co-producing governance and intervention logic; and (iv) defining a feasible measurement architecture. Co-design workshops with an international group of 23 stakeholders from different development sectors and disciplinary backgrounds revealed that while health is a primary researcher objective, road safety and personal security are the primary locally credible entry points for institutional engagement in Maputo and Marracuene.
Key outputs include a tailored CFIR determinant map and a minimum-viable measurement architecture that links implementation outcomes, specifically acceptability, feasibility, and adaptability, to physical activity and well-being. By documenting how implementation determinants were negotiated across fragmented governance structures, this paper contributes a framework for transport practitioners in Sub-Saharan Africa. The OWS pathway moves beyond evaluating intervention effectiveness to studying the processes of delivery. It provides a technical blueprint for designing active travel interventions in urban and peri-urban resource-constrained contexts.""" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10533>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8636> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "On the way to school: Co-producing pathways for evidence-based implementation of active mobility to school interventions" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2950196226000190"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137496> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137496 ;
    nex:description "This paper presents the development of analytical fragility and vulnerability functions for flexure-controlled partially grouted reinforced masonry (RM) buildings that are representative of Colombian urban areas, with the goal of enhancing national seismic risk assessments. An inventory was compiled from the 2019 national census, and 36 representative RM buildings were identified to capture regional variations in height, wall density, and reinforcement ratios. Fourteen archetype buildings from the inventory were modeled using three-dimensional macro-element approaches implemented in the OpenSees platform. Each archetype model was subjected to at least 600 ground motion records that were representatives of Colombian seismic sources, and this resulted in a large dataset of engineering demand parameters. Fragility functions were derived for multiple damage states within the PEER-PBEE probabilistic framework, and corresponding vulnerability functions were established in terms of expected economic losses. The derived curves showed reasonable agreement with results from the literature, including post-earthquake empirical fragility functions and published functions for RM. The analyses indicate that taller RM buildings tend to sustain damage at lower ground motion intensities compared to shorter ones, and that RM buildings in Colombia exhibit higher loss estimates than those reported in previous regional studies. The results provide validated fragility and vulnerability functions for Colombian RM construction, contributing to the ongoing development of the country’s national seismic risk model and to the development targeted mitigation strategies." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10104>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/11401> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Seismic fragility and vulnerability of partially grouted flexural-controlled reinforced masonry buildings in Colombia" ;
    nex:url "http://dx.doi.org/10.1002/esp4.70080"^^xsd:anyURI .

