<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137952> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137952 ;
    nex:description "El objetivo de este artículo es contribuir a la reconstrucción de los hechos vinculados al funcionamiento de las Unidades Permanentes de Justicia (UPJ) en Colombia a través del estudio de caso de la UPJ de Puente Aranda en la ciudad de Bogotá D.C. Para la elaboración de este estudio tomamos como fundamentos teóricos los trabajos desarrollados en el campo criminológico sobre la discrecionalidad y la selectividad de la actividad policial y del sistema de justicia. La metodología de esta investigación es cualitativa. Realizamos entrevistas a personas que fueron detenidas en ese lugar." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/57141>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/89379> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "La Unidad Permanente de Justicia de Puente Aranda: Discrecionalidad, selectividad y trabajo policial" ;
    nex:url "https://revistas.uniandes.edu.co/index.php/lar/article/view/10898/10824"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/138099> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 138099 ;
    nex:description "Additive manufacturing enables the fabrication of lightweight cellular lattices with tailorable mechanical behavior. This study examines the low-velocity impact response of thermoplastic polyurethane (TPU) honeycombs produced by fused filament fabrication (FFF) and tested using an Open Hopkinson Pressure Bar (OHPB) system. Five topologies—square (Sqr), hexagonal (Hex-0° and Hex-90°), and re-entrant (Re-ent-0° and Re-ent-90°)—were designed with the same volume fraction and evaluated through force–displacement analysis at a nominal impact velocity of 9 m/s. The square topology exhibited the highest peak force, about 80% greater than the auxetic Re-ent-0° configuration, while the rotated hexagonal lattice (Hex-90°) demonstrated the highest energy absorption, reaching values approximately 60% higher than Re-ent-0°—while maintaining moderate stiffness. Re-entrant lattices provided more compliant responses dominated by folding and buckling, whereas square and hexagonal lattices resisted higher loads over shorter displacement ranges. All structures showed substantial shape recovery after impact, indicating the predominantly elastic and reversible response of the TPU walls under impact loading. These experimental results highlight the influence of lattice topology on the stiffness–energy dissipation balance in flexible honeycombs, demonstrating their potential for reusable energy-absorbing applications such as footwear, protective equipment, and soft robotics." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/74505>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7697> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Flexible Additively Manufactured Honeycombs Under Impact Loading: Effect of Topology and Orientation" ;
    nex:url "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/amse/1234886"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/138110> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 138110 ;
    nex:description "Drawing on more than two decades of engagement with Fernando Pessoa’s posthumous archive, this paper reflects on transcription as a central yet under-theorized practice in textual scholarship. It takes The Duke of Parma as its main case study, highlighting the challenges posed by unfinished and unstable materials. While editing is often understood as involving the collection of sources, the establishment of a text, and its annotation, the phase of constitutio textus is frequently reduced to a technical operation. This paper argues instead that transcription is a deeply interpretative act, far removed from mechanical reproduction. Through examples from the Duke of Parma corpus, it examines editorial interventions often subsumed under emendation. By foregrounding transcription as a site of decision-making, uncertainty, and hypothesis, the paper clarifies the epistemological stakes of transforming archival documents into readable texts." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/89250>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9857> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "The road to <madness> nowhere: transcription and hypothesis in The Duke of Parma" ;
    nex:url "https://repository.library.brown.edu/studio/item/bdr:w8xe628f/PDF/"^^xsd:anyURI .

