<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/138742> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 138742 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "Access to essential infrastructure services such as water, sanitation, and garbage collection can considerably affect children’s environment and may play a significant role in shaping early childhood developmental and health outcomes. Using data from the Multiple Indicator Cluster Surveys (MICS) and the Demographic and Health Surveys (DHS) for 18 countries in Latin America and the Caribbean (LAC), we show a significant positive association between access to water and sanitation and early childhood development, as well as reduced instances of stunting. In addition, we identify a negative association between access to improved garbage collection services and the rates of stunting among children under five. Our findings are robust after using alternative measures for access and controlling for individual, maternal, and household factors, alongside considerations of household wealth, caregiver’s stimulation activities and behaviors, and local community spillovers. Similarly, the economic relevance of the relationship is highlighted by the substantial gap relative to the size of the vulnerable groups, persisting even after adjusting for confounding variables. Our results also suggest that households may be able to lessen the potential impact of pollutants through mitigation measures such as treating water to make it safe for consumption, using handwashing cleansers, and storing household trash in lidded containers. The current findings underscore the importance of investing in basic infrastructure services as a critical component of comprehensive strategies to enhance early childhood development and health in low- and middle-income countries. We emphasize the importance of considering the quality and type of infrastructure services alongside their availability. Future research should incorporate more complete and detailed data to improve understanding of the causal relationship between water, sanitation, and garbage collection and early childhood development, as well as the mechanisms underlying the observed associations." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/29267>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/29883> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Infrastructure Services and Early Childhood Development in Latin America and the Caribbean: Water, Sanitation, and Garbage Collection" ;
    nex:url "id.elsevier.com/as/authorization.oauth2?platSite=SD%2Fscience&additionalPlatSites=GH%2Fgeneralhospital%2CLS%2FLS%2CMDY%2Fmendeley%2CSC%2Fscopus%2CRX%2Freaxys&scope=openid%20email%20profile%20els_auth_info%20els_idp_info%20els_idp_analytics_attrs%20urn%3Acom%3Aelsevier%3Aidp%3Apolicy%3Aproduct%3Ainst_assoc&response_type=code&redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.sciencedirect.com%2Fuser%2Fidentity%2Flanding&authType=SINGLE_SIGN_IN&prompt=none&client_id=SDFE-v4&state=retryCounter%3D0%26csrfToken%3D602ac78c-0357-43c8-9843-c3871631be48%26idpPolicy%3Durn%253Acom%253Aelsevier%253Aidp%253Apolicy%253Aproduct%253Ainst_assoc%26returnUrl%3D%252Fscience%252Farticle%252Fpii%252FS0305750X24002870%26migrated%3DMIGRATED%26prompt%3Dnone%26cid%3Darp-cc612d75-7e1d-4575-aac4-db6c243458dc"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/138826> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 138826 ;
    nex:description "Herein, we report a piperidine-mediated Knoevenagel condensation of (hetero)aromatic aldehydes 1a–f with 2-cyanoacetic acid 2 in ethanol conducted under reflux or ambient conditions, affording (E)-2-cyano-3-(het)arylacrylic acids 3a–f in good yields (46–80%). The molecular structure of compounds 3a–f were established by IR, NMR, and HRMS analyses. Considering the established fluorescence characteristics of quinolin-2(1H)-one derivatives, the photophysical properties of quinolin-2(1H)-one-based acrylic acids 3e and 3f were investigated. Notably, the 6,7-dimethoxyquinolin-2(1H)-one derivative 3f exhibited intense fluorescence emission (ϕF = 0.257), representing a 9.9-fold enhancement relative to the unsubstituted analogue 3e (ϕF = 0.026) in dimethylsulfoxide. These results highlight the pronounced influence of methoxy substitution on the electronic structure and emissive behavior of quinolin-2(1H)-one-based acrylic acids. In addition, the molecular structure and (E)-stereochemistry of 2-cyano-3-(quinolin-4-yl)acrylic acid 3c were unambiguously confirmed by single-crystal X-ray diffraction. Experimental and computational analyses for compound 3c revealed that crystal packing is primarily directed by a strong O-H···N hydrogen bond between the carboxylic acid group and the pyridinic nitrogen (Etot = −53.6 kJ mol−1). This key interaction is further reinforced by C-H···O hydrogen bonds and π···π stacking contacts. Despite exhibiting the most negative electrostatic potential within the molecule, the cyano group does not act as the preferred hydrogen-bond acceptor, whereas the pyridinic nitrogen plays the dominant role in directing the supramolecular assembly. DFT calculations provided insight into the frontier molecular orbitals and global reactivity descriptors, revealing a donor–acceptor electronic architecture characterized by HOMO delocalization over the (hetero)aromatic framework and LUMO localization on the α,β-unsaturated carboxylic acid fragment. This orbital distribution supports an intramolecular charge-transfer pathway across the π-conjugated system and provides a molecular rationale for the pronounced solvatochromic response and enhanced fluorescence properties of compound 3f." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/29982>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/43204> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2027 ;
    nex:title "Synthesis, structural characterization and photophysical studies of (E)-2-cyano-3-(het)arylacrylic acids" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S002228602602288X?via%3Dihub"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/138827> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 138827 ;
    nex:description "Climate-related risks pose systemic challenges for insurance and reinsurance companies, exposing protection gaps and threatening financial stability. Parametric insurance provides a mechanism for transferring these risks through index-based payouts, but portfolio solvency depends critically on accurately estimating exposure to underlying climate drivers. We argue that associational models are unreliable under anthropogenic climate change due to non-stationarity and latent confounding, and propose a causal factor investing framework based on Structural Causal Models and causal discovery. Using the PCMCI algorithm, we identify lagged causal links between large-scale climate drivers and local weather indices, estimate total causal effects along direct and mediated pathways, and incorporate them into a minimum-variance portfolio optimization problem with a zero systematic exposure constraint. We evaluate the framework in two case studies: a multi-peril portfolio across nine U.S. NOAA climate regions and a precipitation portfolio across 19 Colombian rain stations. In both cases, the causal portfolio outperforms associational benchmarks and an unconstrained minimum-variance allocation across multiple risk metrics, demonstrating the practical value of causal identification for weather index insurance portfolio construction." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10850>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/67666> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Causal Analysis for Risk Management of Weather Index Insurance Portfolios under Climate Change" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00477-026-03370-4"^^xsd:anyURI .

