<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/140239> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 140239 ;
    nex:description """Colombia is one of the world’s most biodiverse countries, home to over 10% of the planet’s biodiversity. Much of 
this richness is found in the Amazon biome, which spans over 43% of the country’s land surface. Despite its 
ecological importance, the region is subject to growing threats from deforestation, leading to loss of biodiversity 
and degradation of ecosystem services. Key drivers of this deforestation include socio-environmental conflicts, 
such as land conversion, coca cultivation, and illegal mining, all of which are compounded by Colombia’s 
longstanding internal armed conflict. Socioeconomic conditions in local communities further reinforce these 
drivers, yet limited access to reliable data constrains understanding and has led to a gap in literature. This study 
examines the links between socioeconomic factors, socio-environmental conflicts, and deforestation across the 
Amazon biome in Colombia. Using different sources of information, we construct a broad set of indicators and 
use econometric methods and machine learning (ML) techniques to identify the main drivers of forest loss. The 
results show that while the contribution of coca cultivation to deforestation has declined, land grabbing has 
emerged as a leading driver. Mining causes significant disturbances to the Amazon biome, but its link to 
deforestation is marginal. Other consistent predictors include poverty and the presence of basic trails and roads. 
The analysis also confirms that the northwestern zone remains the most threatened subregion, and that forest 
conservation is positively associated with the presence of Indigenous communities.""" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/66684>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9036> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2027 ;
    nex:title "Deforestation in the Colombian amazon biome: socioeconomic and socio-environmental conflict drivers" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0305750X26002640?fbclid=IwY2xjawUw7N9leHRuA2FlbQIxMQBwZG9mBWJyaWQRMUxIOG04M0d1czgyZGw0R2RzcnRjBmFwcF9pZBAyMjIwMzkxNzg4MjAwODkyAAEemUvksj36dT7l4AJUfZtkMbUORqXULIv-ppeEDMdhIkAxzArIUsq-FB6VFm4_aem_e1sGfwuqwFXRpDJhBcgPyg"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/140248> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 140248 ;
    nex:description "A recent adversarial collaboration on social evaluation has united a number of competing models under a bi-dimensional structure of social evaluation. This proposed structure posits that social evaluation is conducted using two independent dimensions: Horizontal and Vertical. In turn, each dimension is divided into two facets: The Horizontal dimension is composed of the Friendliness and Morality facets, and the Vertical dimension is composed of the Ability and Assertiveness facets. Although commendable, direct evidence for this proposed model remains scarce. Specifically, the literature fails to test for plausible competing models that separate Friendliness and Morality into independent dimensions. Across two preregistered studies (n = 681) and a re-analysis of six recently published datasets (Barbedor et al. 2024, n = 1265; Koch et al. 2024, n = 6368), we systematically compared theoretical models of social evaluation in the context of regional stereotypes (Study 1), cross-national stereotypes in Latin America (Study 2), arbitrary social groups (Studies 3–7 and 9) and individual targets (Studies 8 and 9). Preregistered results provide mixed evidence for bi-, tri- and four-dimensional models of social evaluation. We discuss the implications of these results for adversarial collaboration, specifically regarding potential moderators of the number of dimensions, such as the evaluated social target." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/11127>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8833> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Testing Competing Models of Social Evaluation in Western European, Latin America and the United States" ;
    nex:url "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ejsp.70089"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/140253> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 140253 ;
    nex:description "Este artículo analiza las transformaciones recientes de las élites políticas colombianas a partir de la etapa inicial de la contienda presidencial de 2026. Mediante un marco que integra narrativa personal, ideología, desempeño mediático y digital y trayectoria social, examina cómo distintas formas de capital político, simbólico y social intervienen en la construcción contemporánea del liderazgo y la legitimidad. El estudio muestra, a través de tres figuras ubicadas en distintos espacios ideológicos, cómo se reconfiguran las élites en un contexto de creciente mediatización, cambios en los alineamientos políticos y nuevas formas de conexión con el electorado." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10146>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10807> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "The Evolution of Political Power Elites in Colombia: A Comparative Analysis of Three Campaign Personas in the Early 2026 Presidential Race" ;
    nex:url "https://www.maspoderlocal.com/index.php/mpl/article/view/political-power-elites-Colombia-2026-mpl65/230"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/140262> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 140262 ;
    nex:description "What motivates people to place trust in strangers—the sense that they ought to trust, or the feeling that they want to? Competing perspectives suggest that trust may be driven either by obligations or by desires. In two studies, we tested the role of these motivational forces in the tendency to trust strangers using a binary trust game in both an online setting (Study 1, n = 241; Study 2, n = 275) and a laboratory context (Study 1, n = 163). Our results confirm that both obligations and desires are good predictors of trust in an online platform. However, contrary to our hypothesis, wanting to trust had a greater relevance than feeling the obligation to trust in the laboratory setting. We discuss the results in terms of the interplay between norms, emotions, and preferences." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/124252>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8833> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Do you feel obliged to trust in strangers? The role of obligations, desires and expectations on trust" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691826009005?via%3Dihub"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/140263> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 140263 ;
    nex:description "Executive functions (EFs) are essential for children’s development and school success, yet the efficacy of EF interventions with respect to their far-transfer effects on untrained domains to regulation and academic performance remains unclear. To address this gap, this meta-analysis evaluates the impact of EF training on EF and far-transfer outcomes in children and adolescents, considering moderating effects of intervention type, duration, frequency, and participant characteristics such as age, development and socioeconomic status. A total of 178 studies and 655 effect sizes were analyzed in a series of three-level meta-analyses. Results indicate moderate effects on EF outcomes, and small but significant far-transfer effects on domain-specific regulation skills and academic abilities. Effect sizes varied by outcome, sample type and by type of intervention, with meta-regression revealing larger effects for clinical risk samples and smaller effects for typically developing samples and those from high socioeconomic backgrounds. Effects on regulation outcomes were only significant under passive control conditions, while academic outcomes remained significant against active control groups and randomized experimental designs. Publication bias analyses indicated small-study effects in several models, with bias-corrected estimates suggesting the primary pooled effects are likely modestly inflated. These findings suggest EF interventions can yield small far-transfer effects, particularly for children facing developmental or contextual risk, though effect magnitudes should be interpreted cautiously. Future research should refine intervention strategies, explore mechanisms underlying transfer of cognitive skills and address individual differences to enhance efficacy" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7076>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8833> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Interventions for Executive Functions in Children and Adolescents: A Meta-Analytic Study on Efficacy and Moderators in Clinical and Non-Clinical Samples" ;
    nex:url "https://online.ucpress.edu/collabra/article/12/1/162294/218341"^^xsd:anyURI .

