<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/140343> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 140343 ;
    nex:description "Transport shapes daily life in dense cities by exposing residents to noise, pollution, and insecurity as they travel. Drawing on a 2023–2024 household survey of 2,448 adults around a high-demand corridor in Bogotá, we examine how the perceived livability of this travel-and-residential environment relates to subjective well-being (SWB). We model livability as a second-order construct reflected in five domains (transport, environment, neighborhood, local services, and security) and estimate an SEM-MIMIC linking it to SWB through perceived health and social capital. Livability shows no direct association with SWB and instead operates through better health and stronger social capital, with health the strongest predictor. Conditions of the travel environment, such as harassment, crime, noise, and pollution along the corridor, are among its strongest manifestations. Livability is most strongly reflected in security and environmental quality. Transport and environmental policies may raise SWB most when they reduce chronic stressors such as insecurity, noise, and pollution, and strengthen social resources, with implications for equity-oriented planning in Global South cities." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10533>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8636> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2027 ;
    nex:title "The indirect city: transport, environment, and perceived livability as pathways to subjective well-being" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1361920926004815"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/140353> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 140353 ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/61826>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8173> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "\"\"Arrebatarle jóvenes a la violencia\": Prácticas de resistencia juvenil para la construcción de paz en Quibdó, Colombia\"" .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/140382> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 140382 ;
    nex:description "The emerging feminist literature on care has impacted social movements and public policies in Latin America. However, little is known about community care and the experiences of its institutionalization. This article analyzes the trajectory of the Community Family Welfare Homes (HCB) Program in Colombia, from its creation in 1986 to 2025, as the main public policy for childcare from the contributions of feminist institutionalism. It identifies tensions generated between the State and community organizations in the provision of care and the crucial role of the collective agency of Community Mothers (MCs) as central actors of care. Using a qualitative methodology based on MC testimonies, the article finds that the HCB program consolidated and expanded in childcare at the expense of not recognizing MCs' caregiving as full-time work. In a 40-year history of struggle (legal actions, strikes, etc.), MC organizations reclaimed care as work, historically conceived as a female “vocation”, in a resistance for the labor rights of caregivers." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/34554>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9834> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "El difícil camino de la institucionalización del cuidado comunitario. 40 años de lucha de las madres comunitarias en Colombia." ;
    nex:url "https://estudiosdegenero.colmex.mx/index.php/eg/article/view/1440"^^xsd:anyURI .

