<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/64253> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 64253 ;
    nex:description "This study offers an alternative perspective to answer how and when the board team contributes to the firm performance. We use an information-processing approach to examine interlocks and experience as sources of information that is processed by the directors according to their gender, which in turn influences their decisions. The base of the study is a panel of 12,091 private firms in Colombia, a country with characteristics of gender-inequality. The results show that interlocks and diversity of experience of female directors and long-experience of male directors positively influence firm performance. The study also shows that some informational resources of the board team have a negative influence on firm performance and that there are contingencies associated with the situation of the firm. The implications of this study set around the importance of considering the information-processing approach as an axis to analyze the effectiveness of the resources of the board team." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/58082>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9565> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2020 ;
    nex:title "HOW FEMALE AND MALE BOARD MEMBERS INFLUENCE FIRM PERFORMANCE: AN INFORMATION-PROCESSING APPROACH" .

