<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/88255> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 88255 ;
    nex:classification "B" ;
    nex:description "One-story concrete moment frame buildings present critical joints since free horizontal face reduce their confinement in the core affecting the anchorage of the top beam reinforcement and producing joint distress. In addition, precast concrete process that involve prestressed beam with different shape, precast concrete columns, construction of the joint on the job site and special construction details such as reinforced topping on top of the beams-joints could affect the seismic performance of the one-story precast moment frames for industrial buildings.   Due to the lack of experimental testing, six full-scale critical connections were cyclically tested in order to allow understanding their seismic performance. Test specimens includes; one precast reinforced column, three knees joint and two interior joint sub-assemblages that were designed based on the ACI 318. All connections sustained 3.5% drift ratio at least with no beam longitudinal reinforcement bar rupture or buckling and very low or no observed damage of the column and minimal joint distress, which is consistent with the strong column/weak beam design philosophy. Performance acceptance criteria of ACI 374.1 was satisfied by all test specimens in terms of stiffness, strength and energy dissipation. Nonetheless, the presence of the reinforced topping slab on the top of the joint plays a role to achieve slightly better strength, ductility and hysteretic performance response." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/54877>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7908> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2021 ;
    nex:title "SEISMIC PERFORMANCE OF FULL-SCALE MOMENT CONNECTIONS OF ONE-STORY PRECAST INDUSTRIAL FACILITIES" ;
    nex:url "http://doi.org/10.14359/51733077"^^xsd:anyURI .

