<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/129164> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 129164 ;
    nex:classification "B" ;
    nex:description "A \"turn-on\" fluorescent probe based on the pyrazolo[1,5-a]pyrimidine–dioxaborinine (PpD) hybrid dye for cyanide sensing was synthesised by the one-pot formation of the pyrazolo[1,5-a]pyrimidine and dioxaborinine rings. This probe was obtained in 42 % yield starting from 4-methoxyacetophenone, and its structure was established by NMR spectroscopy (1H, 13C, and 19F), HRMS analysis, and X-ray crystallography (XRC). Likewise, its spectroscopic properties with several anions via fluorometric titration in acetonitrile:Tris-buffered solutions were investigated. The probe exhibited high selectivity to cyanide (CN−) with a fluorescence enhancement against other competing anions (i.e., F−, Cl−, I−, Br−, SO42−, NO3−, NO2−, ClO3−, IO4−, HCO3−, SCN−, HO−, HS−, and AcO−) and good sensitivity (LOD = 0.33 μmol⋅L⁻¹), such as other reported cyanide chemosensors containing the pyrazolo[1,5-a]pyrimidine core." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/43204>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/55505>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9890> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Fluorimetric detection of cyanide using a pyrazolo[1,5-a]pyrimidine–dioxaborinine hybrid dye as a highly selective and sensitive chemosensor" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022286024038092"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/129248> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 129248 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "Water distribution networks (WDNs) are designed under parameters and restrictions that guarantee compliance with hydraulic and water quality conditions in the system. The optimized design of WDNs ensures a lower cost for the network, while maintaining adequate performance to supply the demand to the users. The optimization process leads to the reduction of some diameters compare with those on non-optimal networks, thus changing the hydraulic behavior and hence affecting the water quality performance. This study aims to evaluate the relationship between optimal and non-optimal WDN designs concerning residual chlorine levels and the formation of disinfection byproducts, specifically total trihalomethanes (TTHMs). Seventeen networks were optimized using Optimal Power Use Surface (OPUS) and for each one of them four non-optimal alternatives were generated using Genetic Algorithms (GA). Network optimization was conducted using REDES, and water quality modeling employing EPANET with its multispecies extension. The connection between networks geometrical characteristics and cost was evaluated. In addition, average chlorine and TTHM concentrations were analyzed in the different configurations of the networks, as well as the relation between average chlorine consumption and network geometrical characteristics. Results indicate that optimized networks exhibit reduced chlorine consumption, consequently leading to lower TTHM formation. Future work will focus on evaluating water quality dynamics, considering event-based operational changes" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10429>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/65476>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9870> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Effects of minimum cost optimization in water distribution networks on residual chlorine and trihalomethanes dynamics" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1061/jwrmd5.wreng-6727 ​"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/129383> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 129383 ;
    nex:classification "B" ;
    nex:description "The compatibility between antioxidant compounds (ACs) and wall materials (WMs) is one of the most crucial aspects of the encapsulation process, as the encapsulated compounds’ stability depends on the affinity between the compounds, which is influenced by their chemical properties. A compatibility model between the encapsulant and antioxidant chemicals was built using machine learning (ML) to discover optimal matches without costly and time-consuming trial-and-error experiments. The attributes of the required antioxidant and wall material components were recollected, and two datasets were constructed. As a result, a tying process was performed to connect both datasets and identify significant relationships between parameters of ACs and WMs to define the compatibility or incompatibility of the compounds, as this was necessary to enrich the dataset by incorporating decoys. As a result, a simple statistical analysis was conducted to examine the indicated correlations between variables, and a Principal Component Analysis (PCA) was performed to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset without sacrificing essential information. The K-nearest neighbor (KNN) algorithm was used and designed to handle the classification problems of the compatibility of the combinations to integrate ML in the model. In this way, the model accuracy was 0.92, with a sensitivity of 0.84 and a specificity of 1. These results indicate that the KNN model performs well, exhibiting high accuracy and correctly classifying positive and negative combinations as evidenced by the sensitivity and specificity scores." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/101099>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10151>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7103> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "Compatibility Model between Encapsulant Compounds and Antioxidants by the Implementation of Machine Learning" ;
    nex:url "http://doi.org/10.3390/a17090412"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/129384> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 129384 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "Acne is an inflammatory disease in which microbial disbalance is represented by an augmented population of phylotype IA1 of Cutibacterium acnes. Various treatments for acne can cause side effects, and it has been reported that C. acnes is resistant to prescribed antibiotics. Phage therapy has been proposed as an alternative treatment for acne, given its species-specificity to kill bacteria, its relative innocuity, and its potential to manage antibiotic-resistant pathogens. Moreover, bacteriophages (phages) may modulate the microbiota and immune responses. Some studies have shown the potential use of phages in the treatment of acne. Nevertheless, the capacity to specifically reduce phylotype IA1 and the effect of phage treatment on skin cells are poorly understood. We assessed the capacity of phages to clear C. acnes IA1 and their effects on cell cytotoxicity and growth in HEKa cells- C. acnes IA1 co-culture. Phylotypes IA1 and IB had similar effects on HEKa cells, causing cytotoxicity and diminishing cell growth. Nevertheless, IA1 caused a higher impact on cell doubling time by increasing it 1.8 times more than cell growth control group. Even though there are no phages IA1-specific, we found phages that have a diminished effect on other phylotypes not related to acne. Phage treatment in general reduced IA1-caused cytotoxicity, with differences in efficacy among phages. In addition, phage purification was necessary to restore metabolic activity and growth of HEKa. Overall, phage evaluation as a therapeutic alternative should include phage-bacteria interactions and their impact on skin cells because of the differences that each phage can exhibit." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10994>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/29986>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8517> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Antibacterial activity and impact on keratinocyte cell growth of Cutibacterium acnes bacteriophages in a Cutibacterium acnes IA1- colonized keratinocyte model" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.crmicr.2025.100356"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/129448> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 129448 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/59221>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/84655>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8608> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Impact of time-based variability on planning reliability in construction projects: analysis of production management systems" ;
