<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/130812> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 130812 ;
    nex:description "Artículo de investigación publicado en ACS Sustainable Resource Management (American Chemical Society), volumen 2, número 5, páginas 691 a 700, mayo de 2025. DOI 10.1021/acssusresmgt.4c00374. Autor de correspondencia. El estudio evalúa la producción de cerveza artesanal desde tres perspectivas complementarias: ambiental, económica y exergética, en respuesta al crecimiento del sector de microcervecerías y a la consiguiente demanda de prácticas sostenibles. El análisis de ciclo de vida identificó las materias primas y el transporte como los principales contribuyentes al impacto ambiental, con 51% y 31% respectivamente, y un análisis de sensibilidad sobre el origen de las materias primas mostró que el abastecimiento desde Brasil en lugar de Europa reduce los impactos hasta en 22%. El análisis económico confirmó la viabilidad de la producción artesanal, con un valor presente neto de 49.422 dólares, una tasa interna de retorno modificada de 16,92% y una relación beneficio-costo de 3,02. El análisis exergético identificó la primera filtración como la etapa responsable del 68% de la destrucción total de exergía, lo que señala una oportunidad concreta de intervención en eficiencia energética. El modelado del proceso se realizó en Aspen Plus, con inventarios de producción de malta, levadura y lúpulo, y análisis de incertidumbre sobre las categorías de impacto evaluadas. El trabajo aporta criterios para la toma de decisiones y para el mejoramiento de proceso mediante avances tecnológicos y prácticas sostenibles en la industria cervecera artesanal. Colaboración entre la Universidad de los Andes, la Universidad de Antioquia, la Universidad Nacional Abierta y a Distancia y la Universidad de Burdeos." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/31212>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/50714>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/53917> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Sustainability Assessment in Craft Beer Production: Environmental, Economic, and Exergetic Analyses" ;
    nex:url "https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acssusresmgt.4c00374"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/130838> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 130838 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "Recent industrial production paradigms have seen the promotion of the outsourcing of low-value-added operations to robotic cells as a service, particularly end-of-line packaging. As a result, various types of research have emerged, offering different approaches to the trajectory design optimization of robotic manipulators and their applications. Over time, numerous improvements and updates have been made to the proposed methodologies, addressing the limitations and restrictions of earlier work. This survey-type article compiles research articles published in recent years that focus on the main algorithms proposed for addressing placement and minimum-time path planning for a manipulator responsible for performing pick-and-place tasks. Specifically, the research examines the construction of an automated robotic cell for the palletizing of regular heterogeneous boxes on a collision-free mixed pallet. By reviewing and synthesizing the most recent research, this article sheds light on the state-of-the-art manipulator planning algorithms for pick-and-place tasks in palletizing applications." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10206>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/38787>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/65700> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Trajectory Planning for Robotic Manipulators in Automated Palletizing: A Comprehensive Review" ;
    nex:url "https://www.mdpi.com/2218-6581/14/5/55"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/130851> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 130851 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "Resilience refers to the ability of systems to with-stand, adapt to, and recover from disruptive events. While studies on resilience have attracted significant attention across various research domains, the precise definition of this concept within the field of cooperative artificial intelligence remains unclear. This paper addresses this gap by proposing a clear definition of ‘cooperative resilience’ and outlining a methodology for its quantitative measurement. The methodology is validated in an environment with RL-based and LLM-augmented autonomous agents, subjected to environmental changes and the introduction of agents with unsustainable behaviors. These events are parameterized to create various scenarios for measuring cooperative resilience. The results highlight the crucial role of resilience metrics in analyzing how the collective system prepares for, resists, recovers from, sustains well-being, and transforms in the face of disruptions. These findings provide foundational insights into the definition, measurement, and preliminary analysis of cooperative resilience, offering significant implications for the broader field of AI. Moreover, the methodology and metrics developed here can be adapted to a wide range of AI applications, enhancing the reliability and effectiveness of AI in dynamic and unpredictable environments." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/27715>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8442>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9944> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Cooperative Resilience in Artificial Intelligence Multiagent Systems" ;
    nex:url "https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10988739"^^xsd:anyURI .

