<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/133767> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 133767 ;
    nex:description "The photophysical, thermal, and supramolecular properties of four pirazolo[1,5-a]pyrimidine–dioxaborinine hybrid dyes in the solid state were investigated by electronic spectroscopy (UV–vis and fluorescence), thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), and X-ray crystallography (XRC). The results obtained were correlated, revealing a noticeable association between the analyzed properties, which are governed by the stereoelectronic behavior of substituents in the structures. These dyes exhibited high quantum yield (ɸF) values (31–70 %) with lifetime (τf) values ranging from 1.4 to 7.85 ns, in which the dioxaborinine (DB) moiety favors better π-stacking interactions through the N-heteroaromatic core, inducing aggregation-induced emission (AIE) phenomena. The thermal stability of the precursor dyes (without the DB ring) increased with increasing conjugation, but no trend was observed for the hybrid dyes due to steric factors." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/43204>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/55505>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9890> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Solid-state photophysical and supramolecular properties of hybrid dyes based on pyrazolo[1,5-a]pyrimidine–dioxaborinine" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022286025024469?dgcid=coauthor"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/133861> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 133861 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "This article examines the role digital technologies play in facilitating inclusive innovation processes. The empirical focus is on a group of humanitarian organizations that innovated to respond to the Venezuelan refugee crisis in Colombia between 2020 and 2022. The data sources include interviews with service actors, field observations, and reports to donors. The information was analyzed using a service ecosystem approach to identify how actors interacted with digital technologies to find new ways to co-create value. Findings indicate that digital technologies enhanced inclusive innovation processes through different mechanisms, facilitating a dialogical innovation process between NPOs and refugees. These mechanisms include managing information gathering, enhancing data quantity and quality, providing transparency in analysis, transforming tacit knowledge into explicit knowledge, and providing an ongoing flow of information. The article concludes by discussing how findings expand the current theory on inclusive innovation and their implications for policy and practice." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10043>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/26124>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7315> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Digital Technologies for Inclusive Innovations in Humanitarian Response" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0148296325005375?via%3Dihub"^^xsd:anyURI .

