<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/136036> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 136036 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "This study examines advantages and disadvantages of methods for evaluating street-scale interventions, using a Bogotá road diet. This case provides evidence on how road diets can enhance active transportation conditions and compares two evaluation methods. Combined evaluation methods can provide mixed performance, satisfaction, and experience results." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10253>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/44005>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7416> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "Comparing perception-based methods and traditional level of service for urban infrastructure evaluation: Bogotá’s road diet case study" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cstp.2024.101354"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/136109> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 136109 ;
    nex:description "Over recent decades, Latin American countries have made progress in implementing care-oriented public policies. A celebrated example is the District Care System (SIDICU) introduced in Bogotá, Colombia, in 2020. The policy aims at alleviating women’s unremunerated care burden through social services, education and recreational activities offered at Care Blocks (Manzanas del Cuidado) in underprivileged urban areas. Here we examined the policy’s effects on the care burden of low-income women through ethnographic observation, interviews and focus groups. Our research confirms that the program has improved the recognition of care work’s value. It also provides marginalized women with access to public institutions, often for the first time. In addition, an unexpected benefit emerged through social networks of support formed by caregivers in the Care Blocks. We argue that although the policy does not resolve the complex social challenges of unremunerated caregiving, the SIDICU is an important step toward creating more equal and caring cities." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/11269>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/27233>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8173> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Care networks as unanticipated gains of local public policy" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1038/s44284-025-00389-6"^^xsd:anyURI .

