<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137363> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137363 ;
    nex:description "Amaryllidaceae alkaloids (AAs) are a valuable class of plant specialized metabolites with diverse pharmacological properties. However, the discovery of enzymes involved in AA biosynthesis through traditional methods has been subjected to several drawbacks over time, demanding labor-intensive screening and optimized growth conditions. Here, we introduce a Support Vector Machine (SVM)-algorithm-based approach that overcomes these challenges by predicting enzyme–substrate interactions based solely on amino acid sequences and molecular fingerprints. We employed a training set of 90 enzyme sequences, equally balanced, where the positive enzymes were selected based on chemical similarity to the substrate of interest (4′-O-methylnorbelladine (4OMET)), and the negative enzymes corresponded to active enzymes toward 4OMET-decoy molecules. Applying this prediction model to transcriptomic data of Crinum asiaticum bulbs identified 19 putative cytochrome P450 enzymes. Functional assays in heterologous systems showed that five candidates reproducibly depleted 4OMET, including a CYP81-like candidate – a P450 class not previously linked to 4OMET turnover. Overall, this strategy bypasses the need for stringent alkaloid accumulation conditions and precise tissue sampling for enzyme discovery, offering a scalable and cost-effective candidate selection alternative for downstream biochemical characterization and pathway elucidation efforts." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/79470>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8299>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8515> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Machine Learning-Assisted Discovery of Cytochromes P450 Putatively Involved in the Biosynthesis of Amaryllidaceae Alkaloids in Crinum asiaticum L." ;
    nex:url "https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acssynbio.5c00779"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137511> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137511 ;
    nex:description "Artículo de investigación publicado en acceso abierto en ACS Omega (American Chemical Society), volumen 11, número 15, páginas 23156 a 23165, abril de 2026. DOI 10.1021/acsomega.5c13283. El estudio evalúa la incorporación de proteína unicelular obtenida de Kluyveromyces lactis en matrices de aislado de proteína de soya para el desarrollo de un análogo de yogur, mediante un marco analítico multiescala. Se prepararon cuatro formulaciones con sustitución de proteína de soya por proteína unicelular en proporciones de 0, 25, 50 y 75%. La adición de proteína unicelular aceleró la fermentación, modificó la organización de la red proteica y afectó la textura y la capacidad de retención de agua. Con 25% de sustitución se obtuvo una estructura más abierta, con poros mayores pero manteniendo la continuidad de la red de soya, lo que produjo la mayor firmeza y consistencia junto con incrementos en el módulo de almacenamiento y la viscosidad, indicando una red de gel reforzada y más elástica; a esa concentración la firmeza aumentó 54% respecto al control. Niveles de sustitución iguales o superiores al 50% debilitaron las propiedades mecánicas, mientras que la capacidad de retención de agua aumentó significativamente solo con 75% de sustitución. Los resultados respaldan el potencial de la proteína unicelular como ingrediente sostenible en alimentos fermentados de base vegetal. Proyecto financiado por Minciencias (contrato 909 de 2021), con participación del Instituto de Agroquímica y Tecnología de Alimentos del CSIC (España)." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/50714>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/66805>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7069> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Multiscale Analysis of Kluyveromyces lactis SCP in a Fermented Soy Protein Matrix" ;
    nex:url "https://pubs.acs.org/doi/full/10.1021/acsomega.5c13283"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137653> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137653 ;
    nex:description "Artículo de investigación publicado en acceso abierto en ACS Omega (American Chemical Society), volumen 11, número 20, páginas 29654 a 29682, mayo de 2026. DOI 10.1021/acsomega.5c13301. El trabajo optimiza la síntesis microfluídica de liposomas cargados con puntos de carbono empleando cuatro geometrías de micromezclador en PMMA (serpentina, SARS, características circulares y cámaras), y vincula cuantitativamente el desempeño de encapsulación con la física del mezclado y modelos de dinámica de fluidos computacional. Los cuatro dispositivos produjeron vesículas monodispersas de 150 a 200 nanómetros de diámetro hidrodinámico. Los experimentos mostraron que la relación de caudales entre fase lipídica y acuosa es el parámetro dominante, con eficiencias de encapsulación significativamente mayores en relación 2:1, mientras que el caudal total no tuvo efecto estadísticamente significativo, lo que habilita operación de alto rendimiento a 150 mL/h. La geometría de cámaras alcanzó eficiencias de encapsulación cercanas al 90%. La comparación entre perfiles experimentales de mezclado y simulaciones en COMSOL Multiphysics, usando esquemas de mezcla y Euler-Euler con múltiples cierres RANS, evidenció que ningún cierre único reproduce simultáneamente el transporte de momento y el transporte escalar en todos los regímenes de flujo. Los ensayos MTT en células Vero confirmaron viabilidades celulares superiores al 70% hasta 400 µg/mL a 24 y 48 horas, con lo que se establece la biocompatibilidad in vitro de los sistemas obtenidos. Proyecto financiado por el Ministerio de Ciencia, Tecnología e Innovación (contrato 624-2022) y por el Fondo de Publicaciones de la Vicerrectoría de Investigación y Creación de la Universidad de los Andes. Coautoría dentro de un equipo de la Universidad de los Andes." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/31212>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/50714>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/99902> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "High-Throughput Microfluidic Synthesis of Biocompatible Carbon-Dot-Loaded Liposomes: Geometry-Dependent Encapsulation and CFD Analysis of Mixing" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1021/acsomega.5c13301"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137673> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137673 ;
    nex:description "We report a Cs2CO3-mediated synthesis of (E)-2-cyano-3-arylacrylic acids 3a–I in 50–90% yields via a Knoevenagel condensation between aromatic aldehydes 1a–i and 2-cyanoacetic acid 2 under reflux in ethanol for 4–24 h. In contrast, 4‑bromo-2-nitrobenzaldehyde 1j exhibited higher chemical reactivity, requiring treatment with 20% aqueous sodium hydroxide for 30 min and affording compound 3j in 11% yield. Single-crystal X-ray diffraction unambiguously establishes the (E)-configuration about the C=C double bond in compounds 3d–3f and 3j. Comparative crystallographic analysis indicates that substituent effects on the (E)-2-cyano-3-phenylacrylic acid framework influence crystal packing primarily through modulation of intermolecular interaction patterns rather than through significant changes in molecular conformation, as reflected in the nearly planar geometries observed for 3d–g and the markedly twisted conformation adopted by 3j. In all examined structures, the prevailing supramolecular synthon consists of centrosymmetric carboxylic acid dimers stabilized by strong O–H···O hydrogen bonds. Variations in hydrogen-bond geometries correlate with the degree of C–O bond length equalization within the carboxylic acid group, consistent with mesomeric contributions that modulate proton acidity and hydrogen-bond strength. In compound 3e, lattice water molecules act as hydrogen-bond bridges, altering the connectivity of the primary dimeric motif. Hirshfeld surface analysis and two-dimensional fingerprint plots reveal that O···H/H···O contacts dominate the intermolecular interactions, while additional contributions arise from C···H, N···H, and halogen-related contacts. Electrostatic potentials mapped over the Hirshfeld surfaces further rationalize the distribution of donor and acceptor regions governing the observed hydrogen-bonding patterns. Halogen substituents display differentiated structural roles: chlorine and bromine participate mainly in weak dispersive or halogen-related contacts, whereas fluorine promotes directional C–H···F interactions and π···π stacking that influence the packing arrangement in 3f. In contrast, the nitro substituent in 3j significantly reorganizes the three‑dimensional hydrogen-bonding architecture, giving rise to a supramolecular topology distinct from that of the remaining derivatives. DFT calculations provide insight into the frontier molecular orbitals and global reactivity descriptors, revealing donor–acceptor electronic structures with HOMO localization on the phenyl ring and LUMO on the α,β-unsaturated carbonyl system." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/27330>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/29982>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/43204> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Crystal structures and hydrogen-bonded supramolecular assemblies of  (E)-2-cyano-3-arylacrylic acids obtained by Knoevenagel condensation" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022286026012913?via%3Dihub"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137702> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137702 ;
    nex:description """Abstract
Perovskite solar cells often suffer performance losses during continuous operation due to interfacial charge accumulation and ion migration-related effects. Here, acetylsalicylic acid (ASA) is introduced as an additive in the one-step deposition of CH3NH3PbI3 (MAPbI3) for inverted (p-i-n) devices.
The effect of ASA is systematically examined by fabricating and characterizing solar cells prepared with different ASA concentrations in the perovskite precursor solution. An optimal ASA concentration of 0.5 mg/mL, yields a champion power conversion efficiency of 20.0% and increases the stabilized power output from 17.1% (reference) to 18.9% under continuous operation. Impedance spectroscopy reveals a reduced accumulation capacitance (C2) for the optimal ASA condition, consistent with mitigated interfacial accumulation and reduced ionic polarization under operating bias. ASA also modulates crystallization in a concentration-dependent manner, increasing grain and crystallite size at moderate concentration while excessive ASA (2.0 mg/mL) reduces grain size and causes a pronounced fill-factor loss. Optical measurements show increased photoluminescence intensity for ASA-treated films, consistent with suppressed non-radiative recombination. In addition, XRD indicates a lower relative PbI2 signal for ASA-containing films. Despite the presence of polar (hydrophilic) functional groups in ASA, unencapsulated devices stored for 120 days show no additional degradation relative to the reference, with both device sets retaining ∼80% of their initial PCE.""" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/37033>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/37049>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7918> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Acetylsalicylic acid as a crystallization modulator for one-step deposited MAPbI3 in inverted perovskite solar cells" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mssp.2026.110738"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137745> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137745 ;
    nex:description """Abstract
Variability in construction activity execution times presents important challenges for planning reliability, particularly in industrialized construction contexts. Key factors contributing to high variability include inadequate coordination of disciplines, poor workflow optimization and uncollaborative work environments. This study uses a case study analysis to examine the impact of building information modelling (BIM) on reducing execution time variability, with a focus on planning reliability. A mixed-methods approach integrates qualitative data from expert interviews with quantitative insights derived from Monte Carlo and Latin Hypercube simulations, applied to real-world project data. Results show that BIM implementation reduces variability across key project activities where planning reliability indicators are often low, such as structural work and finishings. These findings demonstrate BIM’s potential to enhance project performance and continuous improvement in industrialized construction, supporting strategies to increase planning reliability through better management of variability.""" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/59221>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/84655>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8608> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Planning reliability in BIM-driven industrialized construction" ;
    nex:url "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/15623599.2026.2643730"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137781> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137781 ;
    nex:description "Malassezia globosa plays a crucial role as part of the human skin’s mycobiome. However, this yeasthas been detected in other niches, such as the gut. Despite being commensal, the pathogenic linkin several dermatological conditions, but recently, chronic diseases such as cancer, Crohn’sdisease, and Parkinson’s disease, among others, have been explored. Lipids can be involved infungal pathogenesis, and this yeast is characterized by a significant lipid metabolic versatility, witha lack of the complex fatty acid synthase (FAS) required for the de novo synthesis of fatty acids, asit relies on lipase-releasing enzymes. Here, we assess lipid dynamics (lipids consumed vs. lipidssecreted) using lipidomic analysis in the supernatant of mDixon media during two growth phases.87 lipids within 17 classes of lipids were identified in three different lipid uptake-secretionpatterns. Some lipids were characteristic, including the presence of glycochenodeoxycholic acid,glycerophospholipids (such as phosphocholine), cardiolipins, and sphingolipids (such as Cer-PI).Interestingly, sterols, bile acids, cholic acid and its derivates, some phosphocholines, fatty acyls,and cardiolipins were lipids consumed over time. The dynamic consumption of these lipids couldpresume an intriguing role in the metabolism of lipid processes in this yeast that could determinethe interaction process and its pathogenic role." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/27349>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7557>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7806> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Malassezia globosa lipidome: The dynamics of uptake and secreted lipids" ;
    nex:url "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/21505594.2026.2613494"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/137887> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 137887 ;
    nex:description """This work describes the synthesis, characterization, and preliminary evaluation of the anti-Plasmodium falciparum activity of a
copper(II) coordination-complex derived from an imidazole-based ligand. This compound was characterized by Infrared, UV–Vis,
Raman, high resolution mass spectrometry, fluorescence, Thermogravimetric analysis, and electrical conductivity allowing the
elucidation of their structure and which was also confirmed by single-crystal X-ray diffraction. From the crystallographic analysis,
it was found that the copper atom is situated in an octahedral environment, with both carboxylate ligands occupying equatorial
positions and the ligands L located axially. Interestingly, the acetate ions act as bidentate ligands, but in an anisobidentate binding
mode, where the oxygen atoms exhibit different bond distances to the metal center, resulting in asymmetric coordination. This
phenomenon is attributed to strong hydrogen bonding within the crystal packing. Biological assays performed to test the complex
revealed low hemocytotoxicity, with values ≤20% at concentrations above 500 μg/mL. When evaluated in vitro against
Plasmodium falciparum (3D7 strain, chloroquine-sensitive), the complex exhibited significantly higher activity than the free
ligand. These findings indicate that coordination to the metal center plays a substantial role in enhancing antiparasitic-activity,
providing a solid foundation for future investigations aimed at elucidating the underlying mechanisms and advancing research on
the anti-Plasmodium falciparum activity of metal complexes.""" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/43204>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/76290>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8738> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "Synthesis, characterization and anti-Plasmodium falciparum activity of a new copper(II) complex containing 2-(tert-butoxy)-6-(1H-imidazol-1-yl)pyridine" ;
    nex:url "https://chemistry-europe.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/cmdc.70340"^^xsd:anyURI .

