<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/138690> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 138690 ;
    nex:description """This study develops an integrated methodological framework to optimize the placement
of speed enforcement cameras on urban road networks under budget constraints. It combines
a two-stage sequential predictive architecture (Random Forest) with NSGA-III multi-objective
evolutionary optimization to simultaneously minimize network-level speeding and severe crash
probability. The framework is implemented in Bogota, Colombia, whose primary road network
comprises 11,561 directional segments currently monitored by 78 speed cameras. The models
predict a speeding index (R2 = 0.46) and the probability of a severe crash (AUC = 0.68), and
feed the NSGA-III solver under deployment budgets of 100, 300 and 500 cameras. The optimized
100-camera solution reduced the network speeding index by 15.8% (2.6 pp) and severe crash
probability by 0.9 pp; scaling to 500 cameras yielded a 27.3% reduction in speeding (4.5 pp).
Solutions are benchmarked against 10,000 Monte Carlo random configurations. The approach is
transferable to any city with segment-level speed data and georeferenced crash records,
and is particularly relevant for middle-income cities. Article currently under peer review.""" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10253>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7416>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/90750> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2026 ;
    nex:title "A multi-objective framework to optimize the placement of speed enforcement cameras on urban road networks" .

