    nex:title "Quasi-two-dimensional magnetic polaron: an exact self-consistent approach" .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/126878> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 126878 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description """Introduction: Parkinson’s disease (PD) presents a significant challenge in medical science, as current treatments are limited to symptom management and often carry significant side effects. Our study introduces an innovative approach to evaluate the effects of gdnf overexpression mediated by CRISPRa in an in vitro model of Parkinson’s disease. The expression of gdnf can have neuroprotective effects, being related to the modulation of neuroinflammation and pathways associated with cell survival, differentiation, and growth.

Methods: We have developed a targeted delivery system using a magnetite nanostructured vehicle for the efficient transport of genetic material. This system has resulted in a substantial increase, up to 200-fold) in gdnf expression in an In vitro model of Parkinson’s disease using a mixed primary culture of astrocytes, neurons, and microglia.

Results and Discussion: The delivery system exhibits significant endosomal escape of more than 56%, crucial for the effective delivery and activation of the genetic material within cells. The increased gdnf expression correlates with a notable reduction in MAO-B complex activity, reaching basal values of 14.8 μU/μg of protein, and a reduction in reactive oxygen species. Additionally, there is up to a 34.6% increase in cell viability in an In vitro Parkinson’s disease model treated with the neurotoxin MPTP. Our study shows that increasing gdnf expression can remediate some of the cellular symptoms associated with Parkinson’s disease in an in vitro model of the disease using a novel nanostructured delivery system.""" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/11334>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/31212>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/50714>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/58631> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "Assessment of CRISPRa-mediated gdnf overexpression in an In vitro Parkinson’s disease model" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.3389/fbioe.2024.1420183"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/126982> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 126982 ;
    nex:classification "B" ;
    nex:description "We present the design, fabrication and discuss the performance of a new combined high-resolution Scanning Tunneling and Thermopower Microscope (STM/SThEM). We also describe the development of the electronic control, the user interface, the vacuum system, and arrangements to reduce acoustical noise and vibrations. We demonstrate the microscope’s performance with atomic-resolution topographic images of highly oriented pyrolytic graphite (HOPG) and local thermopower measurements in the semimetal Bi2Te3. Our system offers a tool to investigate the relationship between electronic structure and thermoelectric properties at the nanoscale." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/51256>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8293>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8741>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/92814> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "High-resolution scanning tunneling microscope and its adaptation for local thermopower measurements in 2D materials" ;
    nex:url "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0304399124000421?via%3Dihub"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/127129> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 127129 ;
    nex:classification "B" ;
    nex:description "Artículo de investigación publicado en acceso abierto en Micromachines (MDPI), volumen 15, número 8, artículo 1057, el 22 de agosto de 2024. DOI 10.3390/mi15081057. El trabajo presenta el desarrollo y la evaluación de un dispositivo microfluídico de bajo costo para la purificación e hiperconcentración de micropartículas y nanopartículas superparamagnéticas de óxido de hierro, fabricado mediante ablación láser de polimetilmetacrilato, técnica que no requiere sala limpia. El desempeño del dispositivo se evaluó combinando simulaciones multifísicas y ensayos experimentales, sobre dos casos de separación: nanopartículas de magnetita frente a puntos de carbono azules, y micropartículas de magnetita frente a micropartículas de poliestireno, a distintos caudales totales. Para nanopartículas, el dispositivo alcanzó una exhaustividad de 93,3% y una precisión de 95,9% a un caudal óptimo de 2 mL/h, muy por encima de los modelos previos que solo lograban 50% de exhaustividad. Para micropartículas, la exactitud alcanzó 98,1% al mismo caudal, con concordancia estrecha entre simulación y experimento bajo un modelo lagrangiano. El estudio identifica la integración de un imán continuo, en lugar de imanes segmentados usados en diseños anteriores, como el factor determinante en la mejora de la eficiencia de separación. El trabajo demuestra la viabilidad de técnicas de manufactura de bajo costo para producir dispositivos microfluídicos de alto desempeño, lo que amplía su accesibilidad en contextos industriales y de investigación. Colaboración entre la Universidad de los Andes y la Universidad de Antioquia." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/11334>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/31212>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/50714>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9601> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "Enhancing Magnetic Micro- and Nanoparticle Separation with a Cost-Effective Microfluidic Device Fabricated by Laser Ablation of PMMA" ;
    nex:url "https://www.mdpi.com/2072-666X/15/8/1057"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/127537> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 127537 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "The Care Block of Bogotá, Colombia, is an urban program that offers services for low-income unpaid caregivers. This study aimed to (i) characterize unpaid caregivers’ subjective well-being, mental health symptoms, physical activity levels, and use of public spaces linked to the Care Block; (ii) identify caregivers’ perceived built and social environment facilitators and barriers to accessing the Care Block facility; and (iii) document the community-led advocacy process to improve the Care Block program. The quantitative component included a subjective well-being and mental health symptoms survey, and the System for Observing Play and Recreation in Communities (SOPARC) instrument. The qualitative component included the Our Voice citizen science method augmented with portable virtual reality equipment to engage participants in advocacy for changes. Participants (median age of 53 years) dedicated a median of 13.8 h a day to unpaid caregiving, had an average subjective well-being score of 7.0, and 19.1% and 23.8% reported having depression and generalized anxiety symptoms respectively. Caregivers reported that the program fosters their perception of purpose, enjoyment, resilience, and cognitive and emotional awareness. SOPARC evaluation showed that most women engaged in moderate to vigorous physical activity. The caregivers highlighted education, physical activity services, and integration of facilities as facilitators to accessing the Care Block program. Poor quality and lack of sidewalks and roads, limited personal safety, and the risk of pedestrian-vehicle collisions were identified as barriers. Virtual Reality sparked compelling dialogue between participants and stakeholders, allowing stakeholders to reflect on an urban program facilitating unpaid care work." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10533>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/11269>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/27233>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8636> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "Urban Care for Unpaid Caregivers: Community Voices in the Care Block Program, in Bogotá, Colombia" ;
    nex:url "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11524-024-00899-z"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/127869> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 127869 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "Artículo de investigación publicado en The Journal of Physical Chemistry B (American Chemical Society), sección de Materia Blanda, Interfases Fluidas, Coloides, Polímeros y Materiales Vítreos, volumen 128, número 41, páginas 10272 a 10285, el 8 de octubre de 2024. DOI 10.1021/acs.jpcb.4c04036. El trabajo valida experimentalmente las predicciones de tensión superficial en la concentración micelar crítica de seis péptidos de tres a cinco residuos, obtenidas mediante un modelo de bosques aleatorios desarrollado en una contribución previa del grupo, en el marco de la búsqueda de sustitutos peptídicos para surfactantes derivados del petróleo en aplicaciones cosméticas, petroleras y agrícolas. Las tensiones interfaciales in silico se calcularon en el sistema decano-agua y se aplicaron ensayos dilatacionales para determinar el potencial espumante. El modelo de bosques aleatorios clasificó correctamente los péptidos según su capacidad alta o baja de reducir la tensión superficial en la concentración micelar crítica, y las simulaciones en la interfase decano-agua identificaron adecuadamente los péptidos de propiedades interfaciales altas, intermedias y bajas. La reología dilatacional permitió estimar el potencial de tres de los péptidos para estabilizar espumas. El estudio establece una base metodológica para identificar péptidos con actividad superficial y señala como trabajo pendiente el refinamiento de las estimaciones y de la correlación entre propiedades dilatacionales y estabilización de espuma. Colaboración entre la Universidad de los Andes y Seton Hall University." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/31212>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/50714>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7069>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9910> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "In Silico Evaluation and Experimental Validation of Interfacial Properties in 3–5 Residue Peptides" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jpcb.4c04036"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/128267> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 128267 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "The fine flavor cocoa (FFC) market offers additional income for small farmers. Despite the potential of several Latin American countries to produce FFC, their lack of standardized postharvest processes hinders compliance with high-quality standards demanded by the FFC market, as these processes have a direct impact on cocoa's organoleptic characteristics. This work supports the design of a collective postharvest transformation model that coordinates the main steps in the cocoa agri-food chain: transport of the cocoa harvest, classification, fermentation, drying, storage, and commercialization. Our methodology includes a modeling scheme combining optimization and simulation models, considering variability in the processes, and covering operational (transport and resource allocation), tactical (design and sizing of the processing plant), and strategic (sales dynamics) decisions across the cocoa value chain. We illustrate this methodology in a case study in Arauca, Colombia. Our findings emphasize the importance of combining these operational and strategic models since their inputs and outputs are interconnected influencing the decision-making within the cocoa value chain. Our study also identifies key determinants for the postharvest design, highlighting the postharvest center capacity, FFC prices, and the market selling mode (export or local)." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10861>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/54334>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/59327>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9179> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2025 ;
    nex:title "A value chain modeling approach for upscaling the production of fine flavor cocoa in Arauca (Colombia)" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1111/itor.13563"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/128304> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 128304 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "The processing of energy by transfer and redistribution, plays a key role in the evolution of dynamical systems. At the ultrasmall and ultrafast scale of nanosystems, quantum coherence could in principle also play a role and has been reported in many pulse-driven nanosystems (e.g. quantum dots and even the microscopic Light-Harvesting Complex II (LHC-II) aggregate). Typical theoretical analyses cannot easily be scaled to describe these general N-component nanosystems; they do not treat the pulse dynamically; and they approximate memory effects. Here our aim is to shed light on what new physics might arise beyond these approximations. We adopt a purposely minimal model such that the time-dependence of the pulse is included explicitly in the Hamiltonian. This simple model generates complex dynamics: specifically, pulses of intermediate duration generate highly entangled vibronic (i.e. electronic-vibrational) states that spread multiple excitons – and hence energy – maximally within the system. Subsequent pulses can then act on such entangled states to efficiently channel subsequent energy capture. The underlying pulse-generated vibronic entanglement increases in strength and robustness as N increases." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/10202>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7012>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8494>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9965> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2023 ;
    nex:title "Energy transfer in N-component nanosystems enhanced by pulse-driven vibronic many-body entanglement" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-46256-z"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/128430> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 128430 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:description "Artículo de investigación publicado en acceso abierto en ACS Omega (American Chemical Society), volumen 9, número 45, páginas 45402 a 45420, el 30 de octubre de 2024. DOI 10.1021/acsomega.4c07415. El estudio aborda las limitaciones de toxicidad sistémica y baja solubilidad en agua que restringen la eficacia de los fármacos anticancerígenos en el tratamiento del melanoma, mediante el desarrollo de nanobioconjugados de magnetita como sistema de entrega. Las nanopartículas de magnetita se funcionalizaron con moléculas específicas y se cargaron con temozolomida o paclitaxel, con caracterización por espectroscopía infrarroja por transformada de Fourier, análisis termogravimétrico, dispersión dinámica de luz y microscopía electrónica de transmisión. Los resultados confirmaron la síntesis y la carga efectiva de fármaco, con eficiencias de 32 a 72% para temozolomida y de 32 a 60% para paclitaxel. Los ensayos de biocompatibilidad mostraron tolerancia elevada, con tasas mínimas de hemólisis y agregación plaquetaria despreciable. Los estudios in vitro evidenciaron mayor citotoxicidad frente a células de melanoma humano A-375 en comparación con los fármacos libres, con captación celular mediada principalmente por macropinocitosis y endocitosis dependiente de caveolina y clatrina. Los nanobioconjugados mostraron además eficacia significativa sobre esferoides de melanoma A-375. El trabajo posiciona estos sistemas como alternativa para terapia dirigida contra melanoma, con mayor especificidad de entrega y menor toxicidad sistémica." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/11334>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/31212>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/50714>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/54728> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2024 ;
    nex:title "Enhanced Delivery and Potency of Chemotherapeutics in Melanoma Treatment via Magnetite Nanobioconjugates" ;
    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.1021/acsomega.4c07415"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/128513> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 128513 ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/11334>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/11373>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/31212>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/9120> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
    nex:publicationYear 2021 ;
    nex:title "Potential Bone Fillers Based on Composites of Cocoa Bean Shells and Poly(Lactic Acid): Compression Molding Manufacturing" ;
    nex:url "https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/9626124"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/128631> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 128631 ;
    nex:classification "A" ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/50968>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7239>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/7533>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/8236> ;
    nex:productType "article" ;
