    nex:url "https://doi.org/10.18389/dearq42.2025.01"^^xsd:anyURI .

<https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/article/133464> a nex:JournalArticle ;
    nex:articleId 133464 ;
    nex:classification "B" ;
    nex:description "This review analyzes the literature on the different approaches proposed for solving trajectory control problems in multi-unit chemical processes requiring transitions between different products or varying purity specifications of the same product. The reviewed studies are classified into two main categories according to the type of tool employed to implement the proposed control strategies: (i) general algebraic programming environments (e.g., Matlab & Simulink®, Python, GAMS), and (ii) specialized commercial process simulation software (e.g., Aspen Plus®, gPROMS®, ProSimPlus®). Both categories serve as platforms for formulating, developing, and implementing the proposed control strategies. A research gap emerges regarding plant-wide control: existing studies rarely consider the coordinated integration of all operating units and the corresponding scheduling actions required to manage transitions among different operating conditions. While much of the existing literature focuses on disturbance rejection, it often overlooks the additional complexity posed by planned operational transitions, where multiple units must be smoothly shifted to new steady states. Based on this review, a methodology is proposed that combines detailed simulation environments with advanced control techniques without requiring exhaustive mathematical modeling of each process unit. However, practical validation of this methodology is beyond the scope of this review." ;
    nex:hasAuthor <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/50970>,
        <https://w3id.org/nexus/resource/professor/66679>,
